// Depth layer: analogy, full explanation, and drill set for every lesson.
// an = analogy · ex = explanation paragraphs · d = drill items [question, answer, alternates?]
export const DEPTH = {

signed: { an:'Think of a signed integer as a position on a one-way street with a direction flag. The number tells you how far, the sign tells you which way — which is exactly what the top bit of a two\u2019s-complement integer does.',
 ex:['A number line is the whole of signed arithmetic. Positive numbers sit to the right of zero, negative numbers to the left, and every operation is a movement along it. Adding a positive number moves you right; adding a negative one moves you left.',
   'Subtraction is not a second operation. a \u2212 b is defined as a + (\u2212b), which is why \u22127 \u2212 (\u22123) becomes \u22127 + 3. Once you internalise that, double negatives stop being a trick and become a consequence: flipping direction twice returns you to the original direction.',
   'Multiplication of signs follows the same logic. Multiplying by \u22121 reflects the number line about zero, so multiplying by \u22121 twice reflects it back. This is why (\u2212a)(\u2212b) = ab, and why in C the expression -(-x) is simply x.',
   'In hardware, this is two\u2019s complement: the bit pattern 11111111 in a signed byte means \u22121 because adding 1 to it wraps to zero. The number line is a circle, and negatives are the values just below the wrap point.'],
 d:[['\u22125 + 12','7'],['8 \u2212 (\u221215)','23'],['(\u22123)(\u22127)','21'],['|\u221219| \u2212 4','15'],['\u221220 \u00f7 (\u22125)','4']]},

'order-ops': { an:'Precedence is a queue with priority levels. Exponents are the executives who get served first, multiplication and division are managers, addition and subtraction wait at the back — and brackets are a VIP pass that jumps the whole line.',
 ex:['An expression is not evaluated left to right; it is evaluated by rank. The ranking is brackets, then exponents, then multiplication and division as one tier, then addition and subtraction as one tier. Within a tier you do go left to right.',
   'The two tiers that share a rank cause most errors. In 12 \u00f7 4 \u00d7 3 the division happens first because it is further left, giving 9, not 1. The same rule governs 10 \u2212 3 + 2, which is 9 rather than 5.',
   'Unary minus is the other trap. \u2212x\u00b2 means \u2212(x\u00b2), because the exponent outranks the negation. If you want the square of a negative you must write (\u2212x)\u00b2, and this exact distinction has produced sign errors in physics code for decades.',
   'Every programming language encodes this as an operator precedence table, and languages disagree at the edges. That is why experienced engineers write explicit brackets even when the precedence is technically on their side.'],
 d:[['3 + 4 \u00d7 5','23'],['(3 + 4) \u00d7 5','35'],['20 \u00f7 4 \u00d7 2','10'],['2 + 3\u00b2 \u00d7 2','20'],['10 \u2212 6 \u00f7 2','7']]},

fractions: { an:'A fraction is a promise to divide that you have not cashed in yet. Storing 1/3 exactly is like keeping a receipt rather than a fistful of coins \u2014 the receipt is precise, the coins round to 0.333.',
 ex:['A fraction a/b represents a divided into b equal parts, taking a of them. Because it is a stored division rather than a computed one, it carries no rounding error, which is why financial and symbolic systems use rational types instead of floats.',
   'Adding fractions requires a common denominator because you cannot add parts of different sizes. Rewriting a/b and c/d over the common denominator bd gives ad/bd and cb/bd, and now the numerators are directly addable: (ad + bc)/bd.',
   'Multiplication needs no common denominator: (a/b)(c/d) = ac/bd, because taking c/d of a/b just scales both the count and the part size. Division flips the second fraction, since dividing by c/d is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal d/c.',
   'Reducing to lowest terms means dividing top and bottom by their greatest common divisor. That is the same Euclidean algorithm you will meet later in the number theory track, doing double duty.'],
 d:[['1/2 + 1/3 (as a fraction)','5/6'],['(3/4)(2/5)','3/10',['6/20']],['(2/3) \u00f7 (1/6)','4'],['Reduce 18/24','3/4'],['5/6 \u2212 1/2','1/3']]},

exponents: { an:'An exponent is a repeat count for multiplication, the way a loop counter is a repeat count for a body. 2\u207f is a loop that doubles n times, which is why a left shift by n is the machine\u2019s version of the same instruction.',
 ex:['a\u207f means n copies of a multiplied together. From that single definition every rule follows mechanically. a\u1d50 \u00b7 a\u207f puts m copies next to n copies, giving m + n copies, which is why the exponents add.',
   '(a\u1d50)\u207f takes m copies and repeats that group n times, giving mn copies, so the exponents multiply. A negative exponent means dividing rather than multiplying, so a\u207b\u207f = 1/a\u207f \u2014 note that the result is a small positive number, not a negative one.',
   'Fractional exponents extend the pattern. Since a^(1/2) \u00b7 a^(1/2) = a\u00b9 = a, the quantity a^(1/2) must be the square root. Roots are exponents, which means every root rule is just an exponent rule you already know.',
   'Powers of two are the vocabulary of memory and addressing: 2\u00b9\u2070 = 1024 is a kibibyte, 2\u00b3\u00b2 is the size of an unsigned int space, and x << 3 multiplies x by 2\u00b3. Knowing the first twenty powers of two by sight pays for itself.'],
 d:[['2\u2076','64'],['3\u00b2 \u00b7 3\u00b3','243'],['2\u207b\u00b3 as a fraction','1/8'],['\u221a144','12'],['2\u00b9\u2070','1024']]},

linear: { an:'A linear equation is a balance scale. Whatever you add to one pan you must add to the other, and the scale stays level \u2014 the same discipline as keeping an invariant true through every line of a loop.',
 ex:['A linear equation has the variable to the first power only, which makes its graph a straight line and its solution unique unless the line is degenerate. Solving means isolating the variable using operations that preserve equality.',
   'The legal moves are: add or subtract the same quantity from both sides, and multiply or divide both sides by the same nonzero quantity. That "nonzero" caveat matters \u2014 dividing by an expression that might be zero is how false solutions appear.',
   'Slope-intercept form y = mx + b makes the two things you care about visible: m is the rate of change and b is the value at x = 0. Given two points, m = (y\u2082 \u2212 y\u2081)/(x\u2082 \u2212 x\u2081) is rise over run, and point-slope form builds the line from a single point and that rate.',
   'You use this every time you invert a formula in code: converting a byte offset to an element index, a frame number to a timestamp, or a screen pixel back to a data value. All of them are y = mx + b solved for x.'],
 d:[['Solve 2x + 6 = 20','7'],['Solve 5x \u2212 3 = 3x + 9','6'],['Slope through (1,2) and (4,11)','3'],['Solve x/4 = 9','36'],['Solve 3(x \u2212 2) = 12','6']]},

quadratics: { an:'A quadratic is the cost curve of a nested loop. Comparing every pair of n items takes n(n\u22121)/2 comparisons, and plotting that against n gives you a parabola \u2014 the shape of quadratic time.',
 ex:['A quadratic ax\u00b2 + bx + c = 0 has at most two solutions because a parabola bends exactly once and can cross a horizontal line at most twice. Which case you are in is decided entirely by the discriminant \u0394 = b\u00b2 \u2212 4ac.',
   'When \u0394 > 0 there are two distinct real roots; when \u0394 = 0 the parabola touches the axis at its vertex and the roots coincide; when \u0394 < 0 the square root is imaginary and there are no real roots. Checking the discriminant first often saves the whole calculation.',
   'The quadratic formula x = (\u2212b \u00b1 \u221a\u0394)/2a always works, but factoring is faster when the numbers are small. Look for two numbers whose product is c and whose sum is b; if they exist, the factorisation falls out immediately.',
   'The vertex sits at x = \u2212b/2a, exactly halfway between the roots, which follows from the \u00b1 in the formula being symmetric about \u2212b/2a. That midpoint is what you want when a quadratic is a cost you are minimising.'],
 d:[['Solve x\u00b2 = 49 (positive root)','7'],['Discriminant of x\u00b2 + 4x + 4','0'],['Larger root of x\u00b2 \u2212 5x + 6','3'],['Vertex x of y = x\u00b2 \u2212 8x + 1','4'],['Roots of x\u00b2 \u2212 9 (positive one)','3']]},

systems: { an:'Each equation is a constraint, like a rule in a layout engine: "this edge is 20px from that one". The solution is the single arrangement that satisfies every rule at once, and an over- or under-constrained system has no solution or infinitely many.',
 ex:['Two equations in two unknowns describe two lines. If the lines cross there is exactly one solution; if they are parallel there is none; if they are the same line there are infinitely many. The determinant ad \u2212 bc tells you which case you are in: zero means not unique.',
   'Elimination scales one equation so a variable\u2019s coefficients match, then adds or subtracts to delete that variable. Substitution solves one equation for a variable and inserts the result into the other. Elimination is usually cleaner when no coefficient is 1.',
   'For larger systems these operations become Gaussian elimination, which is the same algorithm your linear algebra library runs. Its cost is O(n\u00b3), which is why solving a big dense system is expensive and why sparse structure is worth exploiting.',
   'Auto-layout in a UI framework, inverse kinematics in animation, and circuit analysis are all systems of equations being solved on every frame or every resize.'],
 d:[['x + y = 12, x \u2212 y = 2. Find x','7'],['2x = 10, y = x + 3. Find y','8'],['x + 2y = 11, x = 3. Find y','4'],['Determinant of [[2,1],[4,3]]','2'],['3x + y = 10, y = 1. Find x','3']]},

functions: { an:'A function is a pure subroutine: same input, same output, no surprises. An inverse is the undo command \u2014 available only when nothing was thrown away, which is why encoding is invertible and hashing deliberately is not.',
 ex:['A function assigns exactly one output to each input. The set of legal inputs is the domain and the set of achievable outputs is the range. If two different inputs ever produce the same output, the function is not one-to-one.',
   'An inverse f\u207b\u00b9 undoes f, so f\u207b\u00b9(f(x)) = x. It exists only for one-to-one functions, because otherwise the undo step would not know which input to return to. f(x) = x\u00b2 fails this over the reals since 3 and \u22123 both map to 9.',
   'To find an inverse algebraically, swap the roles of input and output and solve. For f(x) = 3x \u2212 6, set y = 3x \u2212 6 and solve for x, giving x = (y + 6)/3, so f\u207b\u00b9(y) = (y + 6)/3.',
   'Composition (f \u2218 g)(x) = f(g(x)) applies g first, which is the same right-to-left order as nested function calls in code. The inverse of a composition reverses the order: (f \u2218 g)\u207b\u00b9 = g\u207b\u00b9 \u2218 f\u207b\u00b9, exactly like unwinding a stack.'],
 d:[['f(x) = 2x + 1. f(5)','11'],['f(x) = 2x + 1. f\u207b\u00b9(9)','4'],['f(x)=x+3, g(x)=2x. f(g(4))','11'],['f(x) = x\u00b3. f\u207b\u00b9(27)','3'],['f(x) = 5x. f\u207b\u00b9(40)','8']]},

polynomials: { an:'A polynomial is a bead necklace of powers, and factoring is finding where it can be unclasped. Once it hangs as a product of short strands, every root is the place a strand goes slack \u2014 the zero-product rule reads them straight off.',
 ex:['A polynomial is a sum of terms a\u2099x\u207f with whole-number exponents. Its degree \u2014 the highest power \u2014 controls almost everything: how many roots it can have, and which way its ends point.',
   'Factoring rewrites the sum as a product, and the payoff is the zero-product rule: a product is zero exactly when a factor is. (x \u2212 3)(x + 7) = 0 hands you both roots with no formula at all.',
   'The patterns worth knowing on sight are the difference of squares a\u00b2 \u2212 b\u00b2 = (a\u2212b)(a+b) and the trinomial hunt for two numbers with a given sum and product. Everything else reduces to these plus patience.',
   'Horner\u2019s method evaluates a polynomial by nesting: ((ax+b)x+c)x+d. Degree n costs n multiplications instead of the naive n\u00b2/2, and it is how every calculator, compiler and checksum routine actually evaluates polynomials.'],
 d:[['Factor x\u00b2\u22129: roots are \u00b1 what','3'],['Roots of (x\u22122)(x+5): larger one','2'],['Degree of 4x\u00b3 + x \u2212 7','3'],['Horner multiplies for degree 8','8'],['Two numbers: sum 7, product 12. Larger','4']]},

rational: { an:'A rational expression is a machine with a forbidden dial setting. Everywhere else it hums along; set the denominator\u2019s zero and it tears itself apart. Asymptotes are the warning stripes painted around that setting.',
 ex:['A rational expression is one polynomial over another, defined everywhere except where the denominator vanishes. Listing those excluded values first is the discipline \u2014 the same one-line guard every division in code needs.',
   'Simplifying means factoring top and bottom and cancelling whole factors. Terms never cancel: (x + 2)/2 is not x + 1, and that single confusion produces more wrong algebra than any other.',
   'Vertical asymptotes sit at denominator zeros that survive cancelling; a zero that cancels leaves only a hole. Horizontal asymptotes come from comparing degrees, which is the limit-at-infinity argument: bottom-heavy goes to 0, matched degrees go to the ratio of leading coefficients.',
   'Performance curves are rational functions: throughput against load rises, bends and saturates at a horizontal asymptote. Reading the asymptote is reading the system\u2019s ceiling.'],
 d:[['Undefined x for (x+1)/(x\u22124)','4'],['Horizontal asymptote of (2x\u00b2+1)/(x\u00b2+3)','2'],['Horizontal asymptote of 5/(x+1)','0'],['Simplify (x\u00b2\u22129)/(x\u22123) at x = 5','8'],['Undefined x for 7/(2x\u221210)','5']]},

'right-triangles': { an:'Sine and cosine are a decomposition: they split a direction into how much of it points up and how much points across. It is the same move as splitting a velocity into x and y components before you store it in a struct.',
 ex:['In a right triangle the angle completely determines the ratios between the sides, no matter how large the triangle is. That scale-independence is what makes trigonometry useful: one angle gives you three ratios you can apply at any size.',
   'SOH-CAH-TOA names them: sine is opposite over hypotenuse, cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse, tangent is opposite over adjacent. Tangent is also the slope of the ray, which is why atan2 is the standard way to turn a vector into an angle.',
   'Pythagoras a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 = c\u00b2 relates the three lengths without reference to any angle. Applied to a vector with components x and y it gives the magnitude \u221a(x\u00b2 + y\u00b2), which is the Euclidean distance formula in disguise.',
   'The 3-4-5 and 5-12-13 triangles are worth memorising; they appear constantly in exercises and let you sanity-check a calculation without a calculator.'],
 d:[['Legs 6 and 8. Hypotenuse','10'],['Legs 5 and 12. Hypotenuse','13'],['Vector (9, 12). Length','15'],['sin 90\u00b0','1'],['tan 45\u00b0','1']]},

'unit-circle': { an:'The unit circle is a clock face with radius 1. Instead of reading hours you read coordinates, and walking around it once is 2\u03c0 radians \u2014 which is the unit every trig function in every standard library actually expects.',
 ex:['A radian is defined as the angle that cuts off an arc equal in length to the radius. Since a full circle has circumference 2\u03c0r, a full turn is 2\u03c0 radians. Converting is one multiplication: radians = degrees \u00d7 \u03c0/180.',
   'On the unit circle, the point at angle \u03b8 has coordinates (cos \u03b8, sin \u03b8). Reading cosine as the x-coordinate and sine as the y-coordinate removes almost all memorisation: cos(\u03c0) = \u22121 because half a turn lands at (\u22121, 0).',
   'The four quadrant signs follow immediately from that reading. In the second quadrant x is negative and y positive, so cosine is negative and sine is positive there. No mnemonic required if you picture the point.',
   'Passing degrees to a function expecting radians is one of the most common silent bugs in graphics and simulation code, because the result is plausible-looking but wrong by a factor of about 57.'],
 d:[['180\u00b0 in radians (as a multiple of \u03c0, enter the coefficient)','1'],['sin 0','0'],['cos 0','1'],['360\u00b0 in radians as coefficient of \u03c0','2'],['sin(\u03c0)','0']]},

identities: { an:'Identities are refactoring rules for trig expressions: they change the form without changing the value, exactly like rewriting a boolean condition into an equivalent but cheaper one.',
 ex:['The Pythagorean identity sin\u00b2\u03b8 + cos\u00b2\u03b8 = 1 is Pythagoras applied to the unit circle, where the hypotenuse is 1 and the legs are cos \u03b8 and sin \u03b8. It holds for every angle, which makes it the workhorse for eliminating one function in favour of the other.',
   'The angle addition formulas describe what happens when you rotate twice. sin(a+b) = sin a cos b + cos a sin b and cos(a+b) = cos a cos b \u2212 sin a sin b. The minus sign in the cosine version is the one people drop.',
   'Those two formulas are exactly the entries of a 2D rotation matrix product, which is the deep reason composing two rotations adds their angles. Graphics libraries rely on this to combine transforms without recomputing from scratch.',
   'Double-angle formulas are the special case b = a: sin 2\u03b8 = 2 sin \u03b8 cos \u03b8 and cos 2\u03b8 = cos\u00b2\u03b8 \u2212 sin\u00b2\u03b8. Deriving them on the spot is safer than recalling them.'],
 d:[['sin\u00b2x + cos\u00b2x','1'],['If sin \u03b8 = 0.6, cos\u00b2\u03b8','0.64'],['sin\u00b2x + cos\u00b2x + 9','10'],['cos 0 + sin 0','1'],['sin 2\u03b8 when sin\u03b8=1, cos\u03b8=0','0']]},

waves: { an:'A wave is a loop that never exits, and its three parameters are the tuning knobs: amplitude is volume, period is tempo, phase is the offset you start at. Easing curves in a UI are the same three knobs on a shorter leash.',
 ex:['The general sinusoid y = A sin(Bx + C) + D has four parameters and each does one job. A scales the output vertically, D shifts it vertically, B compresses horizontally and C shifts horizontally.',
   'Period is 2\u03c0/B: a larger B squeezes more cycles into the same span, which raises the frequency. Frequency f = 1/T is the reciprocal of period, so a 0.005-second period is 200 hertz.',
   'Digital audio is this idea sampled: a 44.1 kHz sample rate means 44,100 measurements a second, and the Nyquist limit says you can only faithfully record frequencies below half that. Aliasing is what happens when you break that rule.',
   'Any repeating signal, however jagged, can be written as a sum of sines with different amplitudes and frequencies. That is the Fourier transform, and it is why understanding a single sine wave generalises so far.'],
 d:[['Period of sin(2x) as coefficient of \u03c0','1'],['Amplitude of 5sin(x)','5'],['Frequency if period is 0.02 s','50'],['Period of sin(x) as coefficient of \u03c0','2'],['Amplitude of \u22123sin(x)','3']]},

'law-sines': { an:'Pythagoras is a specialist who only works on right angles. The law of cosines is his worldly cousin: same formula, plus a correction term that measures exactly how far from 90\u00b0 the angle strays \u2014 and vanishes politely when the specialist would have sufficed.',
 ex:['Most triangles have no right angle, so SOH-CAH-TOA does not apply directly. The two laws fill the gap: sines when you have angle-side pairs, cosines when you have two sides and the angle between them.',
   'The law of sines says a/sin A is the same for all three pairs \u2014 each side is proportional to the sine of its opposite angle. Bigger angle, longer opposite side, in exact proportion.',
   'The law of cosines c\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 \u2212 2ab cos C generalises Pythagoras. At C = 90\u00b0 the cosine is zero and the correction disappears; acute angles shrink c, obtuse angles stretch it.',
   'Triangulation \u2014 fixing a position from two known points and measured angles \u2014 is these laws applied. Surveying, GPS refinement and the triangle meshes in every 3D model solve them constantly.'],
 d:[['c\u00b2 = a\u00b2+b\u00b2\u22122ab cos C at C=90\u00b0, a=6, b=8. c','10'],['cos 90\u00b0','0'],['Area \u00bd ab sin C: a=4, b=5, sin C=1','10'],['a/sin A constant: more sides equal it? enter 3','3'],['c\u00b2 at a=b=1, cos C=0','2']]},

'inverse-trig': { an:'Inverse trig is caller ID for angles. Sine tells you what the angle did; arcsine tells you who called \u2014 but several angles share the same voice, so the function must pick one representative, and atan2 is the version that checks both lines before answering.',
 ex:['sin\u207b\u00b9, cos\u207b\u00b9 and tan\u207b\u00b9 answer the reverse question: which angle produced this value? Since infinitely many angles share each sine, each inverse restricts itself to one agreed range \u2014 \u2212\u03c0/2 to \u03c0/2 for arcsine and arctangent.',
   'That restriction means round trips are asymmetric: sin(sin\u207b\u00b9 x) = x always, but sin\u207b\u00b9(sin \u03b8) returns the representative, not necessarily \u03b8. Forgetting this is a steady source of angle bugs.',
   'Plain arctangent takes the ratio y/x and therefore cannot tell (1,1) from (\u22121,\u22121) \u2014 the ratio is identical but the directions are opposite. atan2(y, x) keeps the two signs separate, recovers the full \u2212\u03c0..\u03c0 range, and handles x = 0 without dividing.',
   'This is why atan2 is the standard way to turn a vector into a heading in graphics, robotics and games \u2014 and why code that uses plain atan on components tends to face backwards in two quadrants.'],
 d:[['sin\u207b\u00b9(0)','0'],['tan\u207b\u00b9(1) as \u03c0/d: d','4'],['Quadrants plain atan covers','2'],['Quadrants atan2 covers','4'],['cos\u207b\u00b9(1)','0']]},

transformations: { an:'Transforming a function is mapping coordinates, the same operation a chart library performs to fit data into a pixel viewport. Scale then translate, and remember that changes inside the brackets act on the input, so they run backwards.',
 ex:['Anything applied outside the function acts on the output and behaves as you expect: f(x) + k moves the graph up by k, and a\u00b7f(x) stretches it vertically by a.',
   'Anything inside the brackets acts on the input and behaves in reverse. f(x \u2212 h) moves the graph right by h, because x must now be h larger to produce the same output. Likewise f(2x) compresses horizontally rather than stretching.',
   'Order matters when you combine them. f(2(x \u2212 3)) shifts right 3 then compresses, while f(2x \u2212 3) compresses then shifts by 3/2. Factoring the inside expression before reading it off prevents the mistake.',
   'This is precisely the data-to-screen mapping every visualisation performs: subtract the domain minimum, divide by the domain width, multiply by the pixel width, add the pixel offset.'],
 d:[['y = f(x) + 4 moves the graph up by','4'],['y = f(x \u2212 5) moves the graph right by','5'],['y = 3f(x) stretches vertically by','3'],['y = (x \u2212 2)\u00b2 has vertex x =','2'],['y = f(x) \u2212 7 moves down by','7']]},

exponential: { an:'Exponential growth is a loop whose body doubles the work each pass. It is the difference between checking every item once and checking every subset: at n = 60, subsets outnumber the nanoseconds since the universe began.',
 ex:['Exponential growth multiplies by a constant factor per step, rather than adding a constant. y = a\u00b7b\u02e3 grows when b > 1 and decays when 0 < b < 1, and the base b is the per-step factor.',
   'The defining property is that equal steps produce equal ratios, not equal differences. Doubling every hour means the ratio after four hours is 2\u2074 = 16 regardless of the starting value.',
   'In algorithm analysis 2\u207f is the cost of examining every subset, and n! is worse still. Adding ten items to a 2\u207f problem multiplies the runtime by 1024, which is why brute force dies abruptly rather than gradually.',
   'Continuous growth uses A = Pe\u02b3\u1d57, where e appears because it is the base whose growth rate equals its own value. That property is what makes e the natural choice for calculus.'],
 d:[['2\u2075','32'],['Start 100, doubles 3 times','800'],['3\u2074','81'],['2\u207f at n=10','1024'],['Start 80, halves twice','20']]},

logarithms: { fb:"A librarian kept a million books and refused to walk the shelves. When a scholar demanded a title, she split the library in half with one question — before or after M? — then halved again, and again. Twenty questions later she held the book. The scholar protested that a million books must take a million steps. She said: I do not count books. I count halvings. The kingdom later carved her rule above the door: the cost of finding is not the size of the library, but the number of times it can be cut in two.", an:'A logarithm is a halving counter. Ask how many times you can cut a phone book in half before one page remains and you have computed a base-2 logarithm \u2014 which is exactly what binary search does to an array.',
 ex:['A logarithm is the inverse of an exponent. log_b(x) = y means precisely b\u02b8 = x, so the logarithm returns the exponent. Reading it as "what power of b gives x" makes most log problems into exponent problems you already know.',
   'The product rule log(xy) = log x + log y turns multiplication into addition, which is what slide rules exploited and what makes log-scale plots readable. The power rule log(x\u207f) = n log x follows from applying the product rule n times.',
   'Change of base, log_b x = ln x / ln b, shows that logs in different bases differ only by a constant factor. Since Big-O discards constant factors, O(log\u2082 n) and O(log\u2081\u2080 n) are the same class \u2014 which is why nobody writes the base in a complexity bound.',
   'Every divide-and-conquer algorithm has a logarithm in its cost because the recursion depth is the number of halvings. Binary search is log n comparisons; merge sort is log n levels of n work each, giving n log n.'],
 d:[['log\u2082(16)','4'],['log\u2082(1024)','10'],['log\u2081\u2080(1000)','3'],['log\u2082(8) + log\u2082(2)','4'],['log\u2082(64)','6']]},

sequences: { an:'A sequence is the list of values a loop variable takes; a series is the running total the loop accumulates. Amortised analysis is nothing more than summing that series and dividing by the number of iterations.',
 ex:['An arithmetic sequence adds a constant difference d each step, so a\u2099 = a\u2081 + (n\u22121)d. Its sum pairs the first term with the last, the second with the second-last, each pair totalling a\u2081 + a\u2099, giving S\u2099 = n(a\u2081 + a\u2099)/2.',
   'A geometric sequence multiplies by a constant ratio r, so a\u2099 = a\u2081r\u207f\u207b\u00b9. Its finite sum is a(1 \u2212 r\u207f)/(1 \u2212 r), and when |r| < 1 the r\u207f term vanishes as n grows, leaving the infinite sum a/(1 \u2212 r).',
   'That infinite sum is the key to dynamic arrays. Doubling capacity means the copies cost 1 + 2 + 4 + \u22ef + n, which is less than 2n in total, so the cost per append is bounded by a constant. The expensive resizes are paid for by the cheap appends around them.',
   'Recurrence relations describe recursive costs the same way. T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n unrolls into log n levels of n work, which is where n log n comes from.'],
 d:[['1 + 2 + \u22ef + 10','55'],['Sum of 1 + 1/2 + 1/4 + \u22ef','2'],['5th term of 3, 7, 11, \u2026','19'],['1 + 2 + \u22ef + 50','1275'],['Sum of 2 + 1 + 0.5 + \u22ef','4']]},

piecewise: { an:'A piecewise function is a thermostat: below the threshold one rule runs, above it another. Most of the interesting functions in engineering are stitched together this way \u2014 the art is making sure the stitches don\u2019t leak.',
 ex:['A piecewise function assigns different formulas to different regions of the domain \u2014 it is an if/else that returns a number. Absolute value is the canonical example: negate below zero, pass through above.',
   'The seams are where the risk lives. If the pieces disagree at a boundary the function jumps, which is a discontinuity; matching the values at each seam is a small algebra problem worth doing every time.',
   'ReLU, max(0, x), is a two-piece function and the default activation in deep learning. Its kink at zero is deliberate: stacking linear functions only ever produces linear functions, and that one non-linearity is what lets networks approximate anything.',
   'Tax brackets, tiered pricing, rate limiters and clamping are all piecewise definitions in production. The off-by-one at a bracket boundary is the same bug as a mismatched seam.'],
 d:[['|\u22127|','7'],['ReLU(\u22123)','0'],['ReLU(4)','4'],['|5| + |\u22125|','10'],['max(0, \u22122) + max(0, 2)','2']]},

binomial: { an:'Pascal\u2019s triangle is a spreadsheet where every cell is the sum of the two above it \u2014 a dynamic program four centuries older than the name. The binomial theorem just reads a row of it as coefficients.',
 ex:['Expanding (a + b)\u207f multiplies n identical brackets. Each term of the result comes from choosing a or b in every bracket, so the coefficient of a\u207f\u207b\u1d4fb\u1d4f counts the ways to choose b exactly k times: C(n,k).',
   'That is the binomial theorem: (a+b)\u207f = \u03a3 C(n,k) a\u207f\u207b\u1d4f b\u1d4f. Algebra and counting are the same fact here, which is why the distribution over coin flips carries the same name.',
   'Pascal\u2019s rule C(n,k) = C(n\u22121,k\u22121) + C(n\u22121,k) says each entry is the sum of the two above: the ways to choose either include the last item or exclude it. That recurrence is a dynamic program you can run by hand.',
   'Setting a = b = 1 collapses the theorem to \u03a3 C(n,k) = 2\u207f \u2014 the count of all subsets, connecting straight back to the power set and to why exhaustive search is exponential.'],
 d:[['C(4,2)','6'],['Row sum of Pascal row n = 6','64'],['Coefficient of x in (1+x)\u2075','5'],['C(5,0)','1'],['Middle coefficient of (1+x)\u2074','6']]},

limits: { fb:"A runner was told she could never reach the wall, since she must first cross half the distance, then half again, forever. She ran anyway, and each half took half the time. The philosophers watched infinitely many steps complete in two seconds flat. Where is the paradox, she asked, catching her breath at the wall. They said the journey was infinite. She said: the number of pieces was infinite. The distance was two meters. Learn to tell the pieces from the sum, and you will stop fearing the word infinity.", an:'A limit is Big-O reasoning made precise. Asking what 3n\u00b2 + 5n does as n grows large is the same question as asking what a function approaches \u2014 you keep the dominant term and discard everything that stops mattering.',
 ex:['A limit describes the value a function approaches as the input approaches some point, whether or not the function is defined there. lim(x\u21923) of (x\u00b2\u22129)/(x\u22123) is 6 even though the expression is 0/0 at exactly x = 3.',
   'When direct substitution gives 0/0, the usual fix is to factor and cancel the shared root. That hole in the graph does not affect the limit, because a limit only cares about the neighbourhood, not the point itself.',
   'For limits at infinity, divide top and bottom by the highest power in the denominator. Every term with a power of x underneath vanishes, leaving the ratio of leading coefficients. This is precisely the reasoning behind \u0398 notation.',
   'Continuity is the statement that the limit equals the function value: no jumps, no holes, no vertical asymptotes. Continuous functions are the ones where the intuitive picture is safe.'],
 d:[['lim x\u21922 of (x + 5)','7'],['lim x\u2192\u221e of (5x\u00b2)/(x\u00b2)','5'],['lim x\u2192\u221e of 1/x','0'],['lim x\u21924 of (x\u00b2 \u2212 16)/(x \u2212 4)','8'],['lim x\u2192\u221e of (2x\u00b3)/(4x\u00b3)','0.5']]},

derivatives: { fb:"Two clerks audited a carriage journey. The first wrote: ninety miles in three hours, thirty miles per hour. The second wrote a number every instant, and at one bend recorded sixty-two. The first called that impossible — speed needs two moments to compare. The second held the two moments closer and closer until daylight could not pass between them, and the ratio settled on one value and stayed. That settled value, he said, is what the horse is doing NOW. The magistrate ruled for the second clerk, and fined the coachman for the bend.", an:'A derivative is the readout on a speedometer: not how far you have gone, but how fast you are going right now. Gradient descent reads that speedometer to decide which way to nudge every parameter in a model.',
 ex:['The derivative is defined as a limit of slopes: take a secant line between x and x + h and shrink h to zero. What survives is the slope of the tangent, which is the instantaneous rate of change.',
   'The power rule d/dx x\u207f = n x\u207f\u207b\u00b9 falls out of that definition and covers most polynomial work. Differentiation is linear, so you handle a sum term by term and constants factor straight out.',
   'The product rule (fg)\u2032 = f\u2032g + fg\u2032 is needed because the derivative of a product is not the product of the derivatives. Checking it on f = g = x, where the true derivative of x\u00b2 is 2x rather than 1, is the fastest way to remember it.',
   'A positive derivative means the function is rising, negative means falling, zero means momentarily flat. Machine learning training loops move parameters against the gradient because that is the direction of steepest decrease in error.'],
 d:[['d/dx of x\u2075 at x=1','5'],['d/dx of 3x\u00b2 at x=2','12'],['d/dx of 7x','7'],['d/dx of x\u00b2 + 4x at x=1','6'],['d/dx of a constant','0']]},

'chain-rule': { fb:"A miller's wheel turned a gear, and that gear turned a smaller one that ground the flour. The apprentice measured the river and predicted the flour wrongly all season. The miller showed him: the river turns the wheel three times over, the wheel turns the gear twice over. You do not add my machines, you multiply through them — three times two, six sacks per surge. Any chain of causes works this way, he said. Follow the rates through each link and multiply. The apprentice later trained networks of ten thousand gears, and the rule held for every one.", an:'The chain rule is gear ratios. If the first gear turns three times per crank and the second turns twice per first, the second turns six times per crank \u2014 rates multiply through a chain, which is exactly what backpropagation does layer by layer.',
 ex:['When one function is nested inside another, the outer rate must be multiplied by the inner rate: (f(g(x)))\u2032 = f\u2032(g(x)) \u00b7 g\u2032(x). Differentiate the outside as if the inside were a single variable, then multiply by the inside\u2019s derivative.',
   'The Leibniz form dy/dx = dy/du \u00b7 du/dx makes the reason obvious: the du terms cancel like fractions. That is not a proof, but it is a reliable memory aid and it generalises to longer chains.',
   'The most common error is forgetting the inner factor. d/dx sin(5x) is 5cos(5x), not cos(5x), and dropping the 5 changes the answer by a factor of five with no visible warning.',
   'Backpropagation applies this rule once per layer, multiplying local derivatives from the loss backwards to each parameter. Vanishing and exploding gradients are what happens when that long product shrinks or grows uncontrollably.'],
 d:[['d/dx sin(3x) coefficient in front of cos(3x)','3'],['d/dx (2x + 1)\u00b2 at x = 0','4'],['d/dx e^(4x) coefficient','4'],['d/dx (x\u00b2)\u00b3 at x = 1','6'],['d/dx (5x)\u00b2 at x = 1','50']]},

optimization: { an:'Finding an optimum is tuning a parameter until the improvement flattens out. When the derivative hits zero, the marginal gain is gone \u2014 which is the same signal a training loop uses to decide it has converged.',
 ex:['At a maximum or minimum the tangent line is horizontal, so f\u2032(x) = 0. Those points, plus any where the derivative is undefined, are the critical points: the only candidates for an extreme.',
   'The second derivative decides which kind you found. f\u2033 > 0 means the curve is concave up and you are at a minimum; f\u2033 < 0 means concave down and a maximum. If f\u2033 = 0 the test is inconclusive and you inspect the sign of f\u2032 either side.',
   'On a closed interval you must also check the endpoints, because a maximum can occur at a boundary where the slope is not zero. Forgetting endpoints is the classic exam mistake and the classic bug in constrained optimisation code.',
   'A standard applied problem writes the quantity to optimise in terms of one variable using a constraint, differentiates, and solves. Fixed-perimeter rectangles always optimise to a square, which is worth knowing as a sanity check.'],
 d:[['Minimum x of y = x\u00b2 \u2212 4x','2'],['Maximum x of y = \u2212x\u00b2 + 6x','3'],['f\u2032(x) = 2x \u2212 10. Critical x','5'],['Fixed perimeter 24. Optimal width','6'],['Vertex x of y = x\u00b2 + 2x','-1',['\u22121']]]},

'product-quotient': { an:'Differentiating a product is auditing a firm with two partners: total growth is what the first partner gained while the second stood still, plus what the second gained while the first stood still. Multiplying their individual growth rates audits a firm that does not exist.',
 ex:['The product rule (fg)\u2032 = f\u2032g + fg\u2032 exists because both factors change at once, and each contributes its own rate scaled by the other\u2019s current value. The one-line check: with f = g = x, the true derivative of x\u00b2 is 2x, while the tempting f\u2032g\u2032 gives 1.',
   'The quotient rule (f/g)\u2032 = (f\u2032g \u2212 fg\u2032)/g\u00b2 follows from writing f/g as f \u00b7 g\u207b\u00b9 and applying product plus chain. The mnemonic \u201clow d-high minus high d-low, over low squared\u201d earns its keep \u2014 the order of the subtraction matters.',
   'Together with the chain rule these three cover every elementary function: any formula built from sums, products, quotients and compositions differentiates mechanically. That mechanical completeness is why symbolic differentiation was easy to automate decades before symbolic integration.',
   'In practice you reach for the product rule whenever a quantity is a rate times a level \u2014 price times volume, signal times envelope \u2014 and its two terms tell you how much of the change came from each.'],
 d:[['(x\u00b7e\u02e3)\u2032 at x = 0','1'],['f=x\u00b2, g=x\u00b3: f\u2032g + fg\u2032 at x=1','5'],['(x\u00b2\u00b7x)\u2032 is what power rule result: coefficient','3'],['(1/x)\u2032 at x = 1','-1',['\u22121']],['Terms in the product rule','2']]},

'curve-sketching': { an:'The first derivative is the direction of travel; the second is the steering wheel. A car can be heading downhill while the wheel is already turned toward level ground \u2014 that is a loss curve flattening out, and you can read it before it arrives.',
 ex:['The sign of f\u2032 partitions the graph into rising and falling stretches; the sign of f\u2033 says whether each stretch bends upward like a cup or downward like a dome. Two signs, four possible shapes \u2014 that is the entire grammar of curves.',
   'Critical points (f\u2032 = 0) are the flat spots, and the second derivative classifies them: concave up means a local minimum, concave down a maximum. Inflection points, where f\u2033 changes sign, are where the bend itself flips.',
   'This grammar is how you read any monitoring graph. Falling and concave up: improving with diminishing returns. Rising and concave up: compounding growth \u2014 the dangerous quadrant for latency. The words \u201cstill falling but levelling off\u201d are a sentence about f\u2032 and f\u2033.',
   'Sketching from derivatives before plotting is a habit worth keeping: it catches wrong formulas early, because a formula whose shape grammar contradicts the data cannot be right no matter how well it fits three points.'],
 d:[['f\u2032 > 0 means rising or falling','rising'],['f\u2033 > 0 at f\u2032 = 0: max or min','min'],['f = x\u00b3 \u2212 3x: f\u2033(x) = 6x is 0 at x =','0'],['Falling + concave up: returns are','diminishing'],['f\u2033 < 0 at f\u2032 = 0: max or min','max']]},

integrals: { fb:"A tax collector had to price an oddly shaped field no formula fit. He cut it into strips a pace wide, priced each strip as a rectangle, and summed them — the landowner objected to the ragged corners. So he cut strips a foot wide, then a finger wide, and the corners' error shrank toward nothing. The sum settled on one number that no finer cutting could move. He wrote in the ledger: when the shape defeats you, slice it until each slice is simple, and trust the sum. His long S still marks the operation.", an:'An integral is accumulation, the way a running total accumulates in a loop. Numerical integration literally is that loop: slice the interval, compute each rectangle, add them up, and shrink the slice width until the answer stops moving.',
 ex:['The definite integral is the limit of a sum of rectangle areas as the rectangles get infinitely thin. That is why the symbol is an elongated S: it stands for sum.',
   'The fundamental theorem connects this to derivatives: \u222b\u2090\u1d47 f\u2032(x) dx = f(b) \u2212 f(a). Integration and differentiation undo each other, which turns an area problem into two substitutions once you can find an antiderivative.',
   'The power rule reverses: \u222b x\u207f dx = x\u207f\u207a\u00b9/(n+1) + C, valid for every n except \u22121, where the answer is ln|x| + C instead. The constant C appears because differentiating any constant gives zero, so the antiderivative is only determined up to one.',
   'In computing, integrals show up as expected values over continuous distributions, accumulated error over a simulation run, and total work under a load curve. Every one of those is computed numerically by the rectangle loop.'],
 d:[['\u222b\u2080\u00b2 2x dx','4'],['\u222b\u2080\u00b9 3x\u00b2 dx','1'],['\u222b\u2081\u00b3 2x dx','8'],['\u222b x dx from 0 to 4','8'],['\u222b\u2080\u00b9 4 dx','4']]},

techniques: { an:'Choosing an integration technique is pattern matching, the same skill as recognising that a problem is really a shortest-path problem. u-substitution reverses the chain rule; integration by parts reverses the product rule.',
 ex:['u-substitution applies when an inner function\u2019s derivative already appears as a factor. In \u222b 2x(x\u00b2+1)\u2075 dx the derivative of x\u00b2+1 is 2x, sitting right there, so setting u = x\u00b2+1 turns the whole thing into \u222b u\u2075 du.',
   'Integration by parts, \u222b u dv = uv \u2212 \u222b v du, handles products of unrelated functions. Choose u to be the factor that simplifies when differentiated. The LIATE order \u2014 logarithmic, inverse trig, algebraic, trig, exponential \u2014 is a reliable default.',
   'For \u222b x\u00b7e\u02e3 dx, take u = x so that du = dx and the remaining integral becomes \u222b e\u02e3 dx, which is trivial. Choosing the other way round makes the problem worse, which is a useful signal that you picked wrong.',
   'Partial fractions splits a rational function into simpler pieces you already know how to integrate. Together these three techniques cover the large majority of integrals you will meet in a CS curriculum.'],
 d:[['\u222b 2x dx (coefficient of x\u00b2)','1'],['\u222b e\u02e3 dx equals e\u02e3 plus what letter','C'],['\u222b 1/x dx is ln|x| + C. Enter 1 to confirm','1'],['In \u222b 3x\u00b2(x\u00b3+1)\u2074dx, u = x\u00b3+? ','1'],['\u222b x dx coefficient of x\u00b2','0.5',['1/2']]]},

series: { fb:"An innkeeper offered a strange tab: the first ale costs one coin, each next ale half the one before. A traveler tried to drink the inn bankrupt, and by dawn had paid one coin, plus a half, plus a quarter — and could not push the total past two coins. Across the road a rival offered ales at 1, then a half, then a third, then a fourth. That inn went bankrupt: terms that merely shrink are not enough — the harmonic tab grows forever, just slowly. Merchants still check which inn they are in before they promise the sum.", an:'An infinite series is a loop that runs forever but may still finish adding up. Doubling a dynamic array costs 1 + 2 + 4 + \u22ef, and because that sum stays under 2n, appending is amortised constant time.',
 ex:['A series converges if its partial sums approach a finite limit. Terms going to zero is necessary but not sufficient: the harmonic series \u03a3 1/n has terms that vanish yet its sum grows without bound, just very slowly.',
   'Geometric series are the well-behaved case. \u03a3 ar\u207f converges to a/(1 \u2212 r) exactly when |r| < 1, and diverges otherwise. Almost every amortised-cost argument in data structures reduces to this formula.',
   'The ratio test generalises that check: if lim |a\u2099\u208a\u2081/a\u2099| < 1 the series converges, if greater than 1 it diverges, and if exactly 1 the test says nothing. It is the first tool to reach for on an unfamiliar series.',
   'p-series \u03a3 1/n\u1d56 converge exactly when p > 1, which is why \u03a3 1/n\u00b2 is finite while \u03a3 1/n is not. That threshold catches people out because the two look so similar.'],
 d:[['Sum of \u03a3(1/2)\u207f from n=0','2'],['Sum of \u03a3(1/4)\u207f from n=0','4/3',['1.333','1.33']],['Does \u03a3 1/n converge? 1 yes 0 no','0'],['Does \u03a3 1/n\u00b2 converge? 1 yes 0 no','1'],['Sum of \u03a3(1/5)\u207f from n=0','1.25',['5/4']]]},

taylor: { fb:"A portraitist was hired by a function too grand to sit for long. She caught its value at one point, then its slope, then the bend of its curve, each in a single glance. From those she painted a polynomial so faithful that, near the sitting point, no one could tell portrait from subject. Far from that point the likeness failed, and she knew it — so she only ever showed the portrait close to where it was painted. Your calculator sells her work: every sine it returns is a portrait, hung close to home.", an:'A Taylor series is a polynomial stand-in, the way a lookup table is a stand-in for an expensive function. Your standard library computes sin and exp this way, keeping just enough terms to stay accurate to the last bit.',
 ex:['A Taylor series rebuilds a function from its derivatives at a single point. Matching the value, then the slope, then the curvature, and so on, produces a polynomial that hugs the function ever more closely near that point.',
   'The series for e\u02e3 is 1 + x + x\u00b2/2! + x\u00b3/3! + \u22ef and converges for every x. The factorials in the denominators grow so fast that the terms shrink quickly, which is why a handful of them suffice for small inputs.',
   'The series for sin x uses only odd powers with alternating signs: x \u2212 x\u00b3/3! + x\u2075/5! \u2212 \u22ef. Truncating after the first term gives the small-angle approximation sin x \u2248 x, which is accurate to within 0.2% below about 10 degrees.',
   'Accuracy degrades as you move away from the expansion point, so numerical libraries first reduce the argument into a small range using periodicity, then apply the polynomial. That range reduction is where most of the engineering effort goes.'],
 d:[['First term of the series for sin x','x'],['e\u02e3 series: first two terms at x=1 sum to','2'],['1 + x at x = 0.5','1.5'],['sin x \u2248 x. Estimate sin(0.01)','0.01'],['3! equals','6']]},

applications: { an:'Every applied integral is the same recipe as pricing the odd field: slice the thing into pieces simple enough to price, price them, sum. Areas use strip slices, volumes use disk slices, averages divide the total by the width. Only the slice changes.',
 ex:['Area between curves is \u222b(top \u2212 bottom) dx \u2014 each vertical strip has height top minus bottom, and integrating sums the strips. Writing the subtraction in the right order keeps the area positive automatically.',
   'The average value of f on [a, b] is the integral divided by the width: (1/(b\u2212a))\u222b f dx. It is the height of the rectangle with the same total \u2014 exactly how a fluctuating load becomes one billing number, or a variable latency becomes one SLA figure.',
   'Volumes of revolution slice the solid into disks: each cross-section is a circle of radius f(x), area \u03c0f(x)\u00b2, so V = \u03c0\u222bf(x)\u00b2 dx. The slice-price-sum recipe again, one dimension up.',
   'The most useful reading is accumulation: the integral of a rate is a total. Integrate requests per second and you get requests; integrate speed and you get distance. Monitoring dashboards do this numerically every time they turn a rate graph into a daily count.'],
 d:[['Average of f(t)=6t on [0,4]','12'],['\u222b\u2080\u00b9(x \u2212 x\u00b2)dx as a fraction','1/6'],['Average of constant 7 on any interval','7'],['\u222b\u2080\u00b2 4 dt (total from rate 4)','8'],['Disk area at radius 2, in \u03c0 units','4']]},

improper: { an:'An improper integral is a rent that never stops but shrinks every month. If it shrinks fast enough, the landlord\u2019s lifetime take is a finite number you can compute today; if it shrinks like 1/x, the total grows forever \u2014 slowly, but forever.',
 ex:['An improper integral extends a bound to infinity by taking a limit: \u222b\u2081\u221e f dx means the limit of \u222b\u2081\u1d47 as b grows. The question is always whether the accumulated area settles or keeps climbing.',
   'The p-test settles most cases: \u222b\u2081\u221e 1/x\u1d56 dx converges exactly when p > 1. The boundary case 1/x diverges because its antiderivative ln x grows without bound \u2014 the integral twin of the harmonic series.',
   'Convergent examples are worth holding onto: \u222b\u2081\u221e 1/x\u00b2 dx = 1 exactly. An infinitely long region with total area one is the picture that makes finite expectations over infinite ranges believable.',
   'That is precisely what expected values over continuous distributions are: integrals over an unbounded range. Long-tail latency percentiles, customer lifetime value and mean time to failure all rest on a tail that shrinks fast enough to integrate.'],
 d:[['\u222b\u2081\u221e 1/x\u00b2 dx','1'],['Does \u222b\u2081\u221e 1/x dx converge? 1 yes 0 no','0'],['p-test: converges when p is greater than','1'],['\u222b\u2081\u221e 1/x\u00b3 dx as a fraction','1/2'],['Does \u222b\u2081\u221e 1/\u221ax dx converge? 1 yes 0 no','0']]},

logic: { an:'A proposition is a boolean, and the connectives are the operators you already write: \u2227 is &&, \u2228 is ||, \u00ac is !. De Morgan\u2019s laws are the refactor that turns !(a && b) into !a || !b.',
 ex:['A proposition is a statement with a definite truth value. Connectives build compound propositions, and a truth table enumerates every combination of inputs \u2014 2\u207f rows for n variables, which is why exhaustive verification gets expensive fast.',
   'Implication p \u2192 q is the one that trips people up. It is false only when p is true and q is false; a false premise makes the whole statement vacuously true. Rewriting it as \u00acp \u2228 q usually settles any confusion.',
   'De Morgan\u2019s laws say \u00ac(p \u2227 q) \u2261 \u00acp \u2228 \u00acq and \u00ac(p \u2228 q) \u2261 \u00acp \u2227 \u00acq: negate both parts and flip the operator. Compilers apply this constantly when simplifying conditions.',
   'The contrapositive \u00acq \u2192 \u00acp is always equivalent to p \u2192 q, but the converse q \u2192 p is not. Confusing a statement with its converse is one of the most common reasoning errors in and out of mathematics.'],
 d:[['\u00ac(p \u2227 q) = \u00acp ? \u00acq. Enter and or or','or'],['p \u2192 q is false in how many rows','1'],['Truth table rows for 3 variables','8'],['T \u2227 F equals T or F','F'],['F \u2192 T equals T or F','T']]},

sets: { an:'A set is a hash set: membership only, no order, no duplicates. Union, intersection and difference are the same three operations a database performs when it joins, filters and excludes rows.',
 ex:['A set is defined purely by which elements belong to it. Order is irrelevant and repetition changes nothing, so {1,2,2,3} and {3,2,1} are the same set. That is exactly the contract of a hash set in a standard library.',
   'Union collects everything in either set, intersection keeps only what is in both, and difference removes one set\u2019s elements from another. Inclusion\u2013exclusion, |A \u222a B| = |A| + |B| \u2212 |A \u2229 B|, corrects for the elements counted twice.',
   'The power set contains every subset, and there are 2\u207f of them because each element is independently in or out. That doubling is why enumerating all subsets is exponential and why subset-sum is hard.',
   'A relation is a set of ordered pairs, and the Cartesian product A \u00d7 B contains all |A|\u00b7|B| of them. A database join is a filtered Cartesian product, which explains both its power and its cost.'],
 d:[['Subsets of a 4-element set','16'],['|A|=12, |B|=9, |A\u2229B|=4. |A\u222aB|','17'],['|A|=3, |B|=5. |A \u00d7 B|','15'],['Subsets of a 6-element set','64'],['|A \u2229 B| if A and B are disjoint','0']]},

proofs: { an:'A proof is a test suite that covers infinitely many cases at once. A counterexample is the single failing test that brings a universal claim down, which is why disproving is cheap and proving is not.',
 ex:['A direct proof assumes the hypothesis and derives the conclusion by valid steps. It is the default approach and the right first attempt when the hypothesis gives you something concrete to work with.',
   'Proof by contrapositive replaces p \u2192 q with the logically equivalent \u00acq \u2192 \u00acp. For "if n\u00b2 is even then n is even" the contrapositive "if n is odd then n\u00b2 is odd" is a one-line calculation, while the direct version is awkward.',
   'Proof by contradiction assumes the negation of what you want and derives an impossibility. It is the standard tool for non-existence claims, such as the irrationality of \u221a2 or the infinitude of primes.',
   'To disprove a universal statement you need exactly one counterexample. "Every prime is odd" dies to the single number 2. This asymmetry between proving and disproving is why testing finds bugs but cannot prove their absence.'],
 d:[['Counterexamples needed to disprove a for-all','1'],['Contrapositive of p \u2192 q starts with \u00ac what letter','q'],['Is the converse always equivalent? 1 yes 0 no','0'],['Smallest even prime','2'],['Is \u221a2 rational? 1 yes 0 no','0']]},

induction: { fb:"A mason claimed his staircase reached the moon. The inspector could not climb forever to check. Prove two things, she said: that the first step stands, and that every step, whichever it is, holds the next one up. The mason showed her the first stone, then one argument — good for any step k — that step k carries step k plus one. She signed for infinitely many steps without climbing past the second. Recursion inspectors use her form to this day: base case standing, each call trusting the next, the whole tower certified at once.", an:'Induction is a loop invariant proved once and trusted forever. The base case is the initialisation, the inductive step is the loop body preserving the invariant, and the conclusion is what holds when the loop exits.',
 ex:['Induction proves a statement for every natural number using two pieces: a base case showing it holds at the start, and an inductive step showing that if it holds at k then it holds at k+1.',
   'The inductive step assumes P(k) for one arbitrary k, not for all n. That distinction matters: assuming it for all n would be assuming the conclusion. The assumption is called the inductive hypothesis and using it explicitly is the point of the proof.',
   'Strong induction assumes P holds for everything from the base up to k, which you need when the recursive structure branches or steps by more than one \u2014 as in proving properties of merge sort or Fibonacci.',
   'Proving a recursive function correct is induction directly: the base case is the recursion\u2019s stopping condition and the inductive step assumes the recursive call is correct. That is why recursion and induction feel like the same skill.'],
 d:[['1 + 2 + \u22ef + n at n = 30','465'],['Base case of induction from 1 proves P(?)','1'],['Inductive step proves P(k) \u21d2 P(k+?)','1'],['1 + 2 + \u22ef + 100','5050'],['1 + 2 + \u22ef + 15','120']]},

counting: { an:'Counting is sizing the search space before you search it. If the space is 10\u2074 you can brute force it in a millisecond; if it is 10\u2074\u2070 no amount of hardware will help, and knowing which you face is the whole decision.',
 ex:['The product rule says independent choices multiply: r positions each with n options gives n\u02b3 total. The sum rule says mutually exclusive alternatives add. Almost every counting problem is these two rules composed.',
   'A four-digit PIN has 10\u2074 = 10,000 possibilities, which a computer exhausts instantly. A four-character lowercase password has 26\u2074 = 456,976, still trivial. Adding character classes and length is what moves the number out of reach.',
   'The critical question is always whether choices are independent and whether repetition is allowed. Selecting with replacement gives n\u02b3; without replacement gives n(n\u22121)(n\u22122)\u22ef, which is the permutation formula.',
   'Counting states is also how you size a lookup table, bound a test matrix, or decide whether memoising a recursion is feasible. It is the first calculation, not an afterthought.'],
 d:[['3-digit codes, digits 0-9, repeats allowed','1000'],['2 shirts \u00d7 3 trousers','6'],['Binary strings of length 5','32'],['4-letter lowercase strings: 26 to the power','4'],['6-sided die rolled twice: outcomes','36']]},

permutations: { an:'Permutations count orderings, combinations count selections. A scheduler choosing an execution order faces permutations; a load balancer choosing which three servers to use faces combinations.',
 ex:['A permutation is an ordered arrangement. Choosing r items from n where order matters gives P(n,r) = n!/(n\u2212r)!, because the first slot has n options, the second n\u22121, and so on for r slots.',
   'A combination is an unordered selection. C(n,r) = n!/(r!(n\u2212r)!) divides the permutation count by r! because each unordered group was counted once for every way of ordering it.',
   'The symmetry C(n,r) = C(n,n\u2212r) says choosing who is in is the same as choosing who is out. Using it to replace C(50,47) with C(50,3) turns an unpleasant calculation into an easy one.',
   'The practical test is whether swapping two chosen items produces a different outcome. Committee members: no, so combinations. President and vice-president: yes, so permutations.'],
 d:[['C(5,2)','10'],['P(5,2)','20'],['C(10,1)','10'],['5!','120'],['C(6,3)','20']]},

probability: { an:'Probability is the expected hit rate of a cache measured before you run it. Randomised algorithms trade a guaranteed worst case for a very high probability of good behaviour, and this is the arithmetic of that trade.',
 ex:['For equally likely outcomes, probability is favourable cases divided by total cases. Getting that denominator right is usually harder than the division, which is why counting comes first.',
   'The complement rule P(\u00acA) = 1 \u2212 P(A) is often the shortcut. "At least one head in two flips" is awkward directly but easy as 1 minus the probability of no heads, which is 1 \u2212 1/4 = 3/4.',
   'Independent events multiply: P(A \u2227 B) = P(A)P(B). This only holds when one event genuinely does not affect the other; assuming independence where it does not hold is behind a great many wrong risk estimates.',
   'The birthday paradox is the CS-relevant surprise: among 23 people the chance of a shared birthday exceeds 50%, and the same maths says a 64-bit hash starts colliding after about 2\u00b3\u00b2 items, not 2\u2076\u2074.'],
 d:[['P(heads on a fair coin) as a fraction','1/2'],['P(rolling a 6) as a fraction','1/6'],['P(two heads in a row) as a fraction','1/4'],['P(not rolling a 6) as a fraction','5/6'],['P(even on a d6) as a fraction','1/2']]},

'expected-value': { an:'Expected value is average-case analysis. Randomised quicksort is O(n log n) expected even though an adversary could force O(n\u00b2), and that expectation is the number you actually plan capacity around.',
 ex:['Expected value weights each outcome by its probability and sums: E[X] = \u03a3 x\u1d62P(x\u1d62). It is a long-run average, so it need not be an achievable outcome \u2014 a fair die has expected value 3.5.',
   'Linearity of expectation, E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y], holds even when X and Y are dependent. This is unusually powerful: you can compute the expected number of comparisons in quicksort by summing over pairs without untangling the dependencies.',
   'Expected value is the right tool for cost decisions. If a retry costs 2ms and fails 10% of the time, the expected cost including retries is a straightforward weighted sum, and that number drives your timeout policy.',
   'It is not the whole story. Variance matters too: two systems with the same expected latency can behave very differently at the 99th percentile, which is usually what users notice.'],
 d:[['E of a fair coin paying 10 for heads','5'],['E of a fair d6','3.5'],['Pays 100 with p=0.01. E','1'],['Pays 20 with p=0.5, else 0. E','10'],['E of a fair d4','2.5']]},

conditional: { an:'Conditioning is zooming a map. P(A|B) throws away every part of the world where B did not happen, then re-scales what is left to total 1. The question is no longer \u201chow likely is A\u201d but \u201chow likely is A in B\u2019s neighbourhood\u201d.',
 ex:['P(A|B) = P(A \u2229 B)/P(B): take the overlap, divide by the new universe. The division is a renormalisation \u2014 probabilities inside B must again sum to 1 once B is all there is.',
   'Independence means the zoom changes nothing: P(A|B) = P(A). Knowing B happened tells you nothing about A. Only then does the joint probability factor as P(A)P(B).',
   'Treating dependent events as independent is the classic reliability sin. Two \u201cindependent\u201d servers on one power feed fail together; mortgage defaults assumed independent brought down banks in 2008. Independence is a claim about the world, and it needs evidence.',
   'The chain rule P(A \u2229 B) = P(A|B)P(B) builds joint probabilities from conditionals, and iterating it across many variables is exactly how Bayesian networks and language models factor a joint distribution into manageable pieces.'],
 d:[['P(A\u2229B)=0.2, P(B)=0.5. P(A|B)','0.4'],['P(A|B)=P(A) means A and B are','independent'],['P(A\u2229B)=0.1, P(B)=0.2. P(A|B)','0.5'],['P(A)=0.3, independent of B. P(A|B)','0.3'],['P(A|B)=0.6, P(B)=0.5. P(A\u2229B)','0.3']]},

distributions: { an:'The binomial distribution is a thousand coin flips summarised in two numbers. You cannot say which requests will fail, but np tells you how many, and np(1\u2212p) tells you how surprised to be when the count drifts.',
 ex:['When n independent trials each succeed with probability p, the number of successes follows the binomial distribution: P(k) = C(n,k)p\u1d4f(1\u2212p)\u207f\u207b\u1d4f. The C(n,k) counts which trials succeeded; the powers price that pattern.',
   'Its mean is np \u2014 trials times rate \u2014 and its variance np(1\u2212p). Those two numbers turn \u201c1000 requests at 1% failure\u201d into \u201cexpect 10, standard deviation about 3\u201d, which is the difference between a normal day and an incident.',
   'The \u00b13-standard-deviation habit is how monitoring thresholds should be set: 4 failures is noise, 40 is a page. Alerting on raw counts without the binomial baseline produces alarms that cry wolf.',
   'When n is large and p tiny, the Poisson approximation with \u03bb = np takes over \u2014 the tool for rare events like collisions in a large hash space or typos per page.'],
 d:[['Mean of binomial n=1000, p=0.01','10'],['Mean of binomial n=100, p=0.5','50'],['Outcomes of 3 flips with exactly 2 heads','3'],['Variance np(1\u2212p): n=100, p=0.5','25'],['Mean of binomial n=50, p=0.1','5']]},

representation: { an:'A graph is a data model, and the representation choice is the same trade-off as array versus linked list. A matrix gives O(1) edge lookup for O(V\u00b2) memory; a list gives O(V+E) memory for slower lookup.',
 ex:['A graph is a set of vertices and a set of edges joining pairs of them. Edges may be directed or undirected, weighted or not, and those two choices determine which algorithms apply.',
   'An adjacency matrix stores a V\u00d7V grid of booleans, giving constant-time edge queries at quadratic space cost. An adjacency list stores each vertex\u2019s neighbours, using O(V+E) space, which is far less when the graph is sparse.',
   'Most real graphs are sparse: road networks, social graphs and dependency graphs have far fewer edges than V\u00b2. That is why adjacency lists are the default and why traversal is quoted as O(V+E).',
   'The handshake lemma says the degree sum is twice the edge count, since every edge contributes to two endpoints. A neat consequence is that the number of odd-degree vertices is always even.'],
 d:[['Max edges, 5 vertices, undirected','10'],['Degree sum with 7 edges','14'],['Max edges, 4 vertices, undirected','6'],['Adjacency matrix cells for V=6','36'],['Edges in a complete graph on 3 vertices','3']]},

paths: { an:'BFS is ripples spreading from a stone dropped in water \u2014 everything one step away, then everything two steps away. DFS is a maze-walker who follows one corridor to its end before backtracking.',
 ex:['BFS uses a queue and visits vertices in order of distance from the source, so the first time it reaches a vertex it has used the fewest possible edges. That makes it the shortest-path algorithm for unweighted graphs.',
   'DFS uses a stack, explicitly or via recursion, and dives as deep as possible before backtracking. Its visit order is what powers cycle detection, topological sorting and finding connected components.',
   'Both run in O(V + E) with an adjacency list because each vertex is dequeued once and each edge examined once. With a matrix the cost rises to O(V\u00b2) since finding neighbours means scanning a whole row.',
   'With weighted edges, BFS is no longer correct and you need Dijkstra, which uses a priority queue and runs in O((V+E) log V). If weights can be negative, even Dijkstra fails and Bellman-Ford is required.'],
 d:[['V=10, E=15. BFS cost as V+E','25'],['Which finds shortest unweighted path: enter BFS or DFS','BFS'],['Data structure BFS uses: queue or stack','queue'],['Data structure DFS uses: queue or stack','stack'],['V=6, E=6. V+E','12']]},

trees: { an:'A tree is an org chart: one root, no loops, exactly one reporting line between any two people. Add a single extra edge anywhere and you create a cycle, which is precisely what a tree forbids.',
 ex:['A tree is a connected acyclic graph. Those two conditions force exactly V \u2212 1 edges: enough to connect everything, not one more, since any additional edge would close a cycle.',
   'Exactly one path exists between any two vertices, which is what makes trees so useful for hierarchy, routing and search. Removing any edge disconnects the tree, so every edge is a bridge.',
   'A binary tree with n nodes has minimum height about log\u2082(n+1) \u2212 1, achieved when it is perfectly balanced. That logarithm is the entire reason balanced search trees give O(log n) lookup, and why an unbalanced tree degenerates to a linked list.',
   'A minimum spanning tree connects every vertex at least total edge weight. Kruskal sorts edges and adds any that does not create a cycle; Prim grows outward from one vertex. Both are greedy and both are provably optimal.'],
 d:[['Edges in a tree with 20 vertices','19'],['Min height of a binary tree with 7 nodes, root = 0','2'],['Nodes in a perfect binary tree of height 3','15'],['Edges in a spanning tree of 8 vertices','7'],['Min height of a binary tree with 31 nodes','4']]},

coloring: { an:'Graph colouring is scheduling with conflicts. Variables that are live at the same time cannot share a register, exams with shared students cannot share a slot \u2014 same problem, different nouns.',
 ex:['A proper colouring assigns colours so that no edge joins two vertices of the same colour. The chromatic number \u03c7(G) is the fewest colours that suffice, and computing it exactly is NP-hard in general.',
   'A complete graph on n vertices needs n colours, since every vertex conflicts with every other. Any odd cycle needs three. A graph is 2-colourable exactly when it contains no odd cycle, which is the same as being bipartite.',
   'A simple upper bound is \u03c7 \u2264 \u0394 + 1, where \u0394 is the maximum degree, because greedy colouring in any order never runs out. Planar graphs need at most four colours, the famous four colour theorem.',
   'Compilers model register allocation as colouring: variables are vertices, overlapping lifetimes are edges, registers are colours. When the graph needs more colours than the machine has registers, the compiler spills a variable to memory.'],
 d:[['Chromatic number of a triangle','3'],['Chromatic number of a 4-cycle','2'],['Colours needed for K5','5'],['Max colours for a planar graph','4'],['Chromatic number of a tree with 5 nodes','2']]},

dags: { an:'A DAG is a recipe: crack the eggs before you whisk them, whisk before you fry. A topological sort is any order a single cook can follow without violating an arrow \u2014 and a circular recipe (\u201cfry before whisking, whisk before frying\u201d) simply cannot be cooked.',
 ex:['A directed acyclic graph points every edge from prerequisite to dependent and contains no cycles. That acyclicity is not decoration \u2014 it is exactly the condition under which a valid ordering exists.',
   'A topological sort lists the vertices so every edge points forward. Kahn\u2019s algorithm produces one in O(V+E): repeatedly emit a vertex with no incoming edges and delete its outgoing ones. If it stalls before emptying the graph, a cycle is caught red-handed.',
   'Build systems, spreadsheet recalculation, package installation, task schedulers and course prerequisites all run this exact algorithm. \u201cCircular dependency\u201d errors are Kahn\u2019s algorithm stalling.',
   'DAGs also power shortest-path shortcuts: on a DAG you can relax edges in topological order and find longest or shortest paths in linear time \u2014 no Dijkstra needed \u2014 which is how critical-path project scheduling works.'],
 d:[['Edges A\u2192B, B\u2192C: valid orders','1'],['Zero in-degree nodes in A\u2192B, A\u2192C, B\u2192D, C\u2192D','1'],['Can a DAG contain a cycle? 1 yes 0 no','0'],['Kahn\u2019s cost with V=10, E=15 as V+E','25'],['Topological sorts of A\u2192B, A\u2192C (B,C free)','2']]},

bipartite: { an:'A bipartite graph is a dance with two lines \u2014 leaders and followers \u2014 where partners can only be chosen across the aisle. A matching pairs everyone off without sharing; Hall\u2019s condition says the dance works only if every clique of leaders collectively knows enough followers.',
 ex:['A graph is bipartite when its vertices split into two camps with edges only between camps \u2014 equivalently, when it is two-colourable, equivalently when it has no odd cycle. BFS checks this in O(V+E) by alternating colours layer by layer.',
   'A matching is a set of edges sharing no endpoint: each vertex paired at most once. A perfect matching pairs everyone. Workers to shifts, students to schools, kidneys to patients \u2014 assignment is matching.',
   'Hall\u2019s marriage theorem gives the exact condition for a perfect matching: every set of k vertices on one side must collectively neighbour at least k on the other. One starving subset \u2014 two workers who both only know one shift \u2014 dooms the whole assignment.',
   'Augmenting-path algorithms find maximum matchings in polynomial time, which puts assignment squarely in P. That tractability is why matching is deployed everywhere from ad exchanges to medical residencies.'],
 d:[['Is a 6-cycle bipartite? 1 yes 0 no','1'],['Is a 5-cycle bipartite? 1 yes 0 no','0'],['Chromatic number of bipartite (\u22651 edge)','2'],['Max matching size, 4 vs 4 complete','4'],['2 workers know 1 shift: Hall\u2019s holds? 1 yes 0 no','0']]},

modular: { an:'Modular arithmetic is a clock. Nine hours after 8 o\u2019clock is 5 o\u2019clock, not 17 \u2014 the numbers wrap. A circular buffer, a hash bucket and an overflowing integer all wrap the same way.',
 ex:['a mod n is the remainder after dividing a by n, always in the range 0 to n\u22121. Two numbers are congruent mod n when they leave the same remainder, which is the same as n dividing their difference.',
   'The crucial property is that mod distributes over addition and multiplication. You may reduce at any point without changing the answer, which keeps intermediate values small \u2014 essential when the true product would overflow.',
   'Hash tables use mod to map an arbitrary hash to a bucket index; circular buffers use it to wrap a write pointer. When the modulus is a power of two both become a single bitwise AND, which is why sizes are usually 2\u1d4f.',
   'Unsigned integer overflow in C is defined as arithmetic mod 2\u207f, so wraparound is not a bug in the language but a specified behaviour. Signed overflow, by contrast, is undefined, which is a different and nastier story.'],
 d:[['23 mod 7','2'],['100 mod 9','1'],['(15 + 20) mod 8','3'],['(7 \u00d7 6) mod 10','2'],['31 mod 4','3']]},

gcd: { an:'Euclid\u2019s algorithm is the oldest algorithm still in daily use, and it works by repeated reduction \u2014 the same shrink-the-problem move as binary search. Each step replaces the pair with a strictly smaller one.',
 ex:['The greatest common divisor is the largest integer dividing both numbers. Euclid\u2019s insight is that gcd(a,b) = gcd(b, a mod b), because any common divisor of a and b also divides the remainder.',
   'Repeating that reduction shrinks the numbers fast and terminates when the second reaches zero, at which point the first is the gcd. It needs no factorisation, which matters enormously for large numbers.',
   'The identity gcd(a,b) \u00b7 lcm(a,b) = ab lets you compute a least common multiple from a gcd with one multiplication and one division, again avoiding factorisation.',
   'The extended Euclidean algorithm additionally finds x and y with ax + by = gcd(a,b). When the gcd is 1 this yields the modular inverse of a mod b, which is exactly the step RSA key generation needs.'],
 d:[['gcd(12, 8)','4'],['gcd(100, 75)','25'],['lcm(4, 6)','12'],['gcd(17, 5)','1'],['gcd(36, 60)','12']]},

primes: { an:'Multiplying two large primes is easy; pulling the product apart is not. That one-way gap is the entire foundation of public-key cryptography \u2014 a lock anyone can close and only the key-holder can open.',
 ex:['A prime has exactly two positive divisors, itself and 1. The fundamental theorem of arithmetic says every integer above 1 factors into primes in exactly one way, which makes primes the atoms of the integers.',
   'Trial division only needs to test divisors up to \u221an, because factors come in pairs straddling the square root. If nothing below \u221an divides n, nothing above it will either.',
   'The sieve of Eratosthenes lists all primes below n in O(n log log n) by crossing off multiples. It is the practical choice whenever you need many small primes rather than testing one large one.',
   'For large numbers, probabilistic tests based on Fermat\u2019s little theorem, a^(p\u22121) \u2261 1 (mod p), are used instead. RSA depends on the fact that factoring a 2048-bit product of two primes remains infeasible.'],
 d:[['Largest divisor to test for n = 49','7'],['Distinct prime factors of 12','2'],['Is 1 prime? 1 yes 0 no','0'],['Smallest prime above 20','23'],['Distinct prime factors of 30','3']]},

rsa: { fb:"A locksmith sold padlocks in the market square and kept every key at home. Anyone could snap a lock shut on a message box — snapping is easy — but opening needed the key that never left her house. A rival melted a lock to study it, and found only the product of two enormous primes; to forge the key he would have to split that number back apart, and all the smiths of all the kingdoms working all their lives could not. She had built a door that closes with one hand and opens with none.", an:'Square-and-multiply is binary search applied to an exponent: instead of stepping through b multiplications you halve the exponent each round. That turns a thousand operations into ten, which is what makes public-key crypto usable.',
 ex:['Computing a\u1d47 mod n naively takes b multiplications, which is impossible when b is a 2048-bit number. Exponentiation by squaring uses the binary expansion of b: square repeatedly, and multiply in a factor wherever the exponent has a 1 bit.',
   'That reduces the cost to O(log b) multiplications. For b = 1024 it is ten squarings instead of a thousand and change; at cryptographic sizes the difference is between microseconds and never.',
   'Reducing mod n after every step keeps every intermediate value below n\u00b2, which is what makes big-integer arithmetic tractable. Skipping those reductions produces numbers with billions of digits.',
   'RSA encrypts with c = m\u1d49 mod n and decrypts with m = c\u1d48 mod n, where d is the modular inverse of e mod \u03c6(n) found by extended Euclid. Security rests on factoring n being hard, which is why key sizes keep growing.'],
 d:[['3\u00b2 mod 5','4'],['2\u2075 mod 7','4'],['Squarings needed for a^1024','10'],['5\u00b2 mod 12','1'],['2\u00b9\u2070 mod 11','1']]},

'mod-inverse': { an:'A modular inverse is the antidote to a multiplication: multiply by 3, then by its antidote 5, and mod 7 you are back where you started. Some poisons have no antidote \u2014 exactly those that share a factor with the modulus.',
 ex:['There is no division in modular arithmetic; the honest operation is multiplying by an inverse. a\u207b\u00b9 is the number with a\u00b7a\u207b\u00b9 \u2261 1 (mod n), and once you have it, \u201cdividing by a\u201d is multiplying by it.',
   'The inverse exists exactly when gcd(a, n) = 1. If a and n share a factor d, then a\u00b7x mod n only ever lands on multiples of d and can never reach 1 \u2014 which is why 4 has no inverse mod 8.',
   'Finding the inverse is the extended Euclidean algorithm: run Euclid to gcd 1, then back-substitute to express 1 = ax + ny. Reduce mod n and x is your inverse, computed in logarithmic time even for thousand-bit numbers.',
   'RSA key generation is precisely this: the private exponent d is the inverse of the public e modulo \u03c6(n). Extended Euclid is the one step that turns a public key into its private twin \u2014 easy with \u03c6(n), infeasible without factoring.'],
 d:[['Inverse of 3 mod 7','5'],['Inverse of 2 mod 5','3'],['Does 4 have an inverse mod 8? 1 yes 0 no','0'],['Inverse of 5 mod 6','5'],['gcd needed for an inverse to exist','1']]},

'fermat-euler': { an:'Fermat\u2019s little theorem is a merry-go-round: mod a prime p, keep multiplying by a and after exactly p\u22121 turns you are back at 1, guaranteed. RSA works by hiding how many turns the ride has \u2014 knowing \u03c6(n) is knowing when the ride resets.',
 ex:['Fermat\u2019s little theorem: for prime p and a not divisible by p, a\u1d56\u207b\u00b9 \u2261 1 (mod p). The nonzero residues form a cycle under multiplication, and its length divides p \u2212 1.',
   'Euler generalised it to any modulus: a^\u03c6(n) \u2261 1 (mod n) whenever gcd(a, n) = 1, where \u03c6(n) counts the numbers up to n coprime to n. For a prime, \u03c6(p) = p \u2212 1 and Fermat is the special case.',
   'Primality testing runs Fermat backwards: pick a random a and compute a\u207f\u207b\u00b9 mod n. If the answer is not 1, n is certainly composite \u2014 no factoring required. Miller-Rabin sharpens this into the test that vets every RSA prime in use.',
   'RSA\u2019s correctness is Euler\u2019s theorem: choosing ed \u2261 1 (mod \u03c6(n)) makes (m\u1d49)\u1d48 = m^(ed) \u2261 m (mod n). Encryption and decryption are inverse rides on the same merry-go-round, and \u03c6(n) = (p\u22121)(q\u22121) is easy with the primes, infeasible without.'],
 d:[['2\u2076 mod 7','1'],['\u03c6(10)','4'],['\u03c6(7)','6'],['3\u2074 mod 5 (Fermat check)','1'],['\u03c6(12)','4']]},

bigo: { an:'Big-O is a weather forecast for code: it will not give you the exact temperature, but it tells you whether to pack for drizzle or a hurricane as n grows.',
 ex:['Big-O bounds a cost function from above, up to a constant factor, for large enough n. f(n) = O(g(n)) means f eventually stays below some multiple of g. The "eventually" and the "some multiple" are both deliberate: Big-O ignores startup costs and hardware speed on purpose.',
   'That is why 3n\u00b2 + 50n + 7 is simply O(n\u00b2). Divide everything by n\u00b2 and let n grow: the 50n and the 7 vanish and the 3 is a constant. This is the limit argument from the calculus track, applied to running time.',
   'The hierarchy log n \u226a n \u226a n log n \u226a n\u00b2 \u226a 2\u207f is worth knowing cold. Each class eventually dominates the one before regardless of constants \u2014 a 1000\u00b7n algorithm beats an n\u00b2/1000 algorithm once n passes a million.',
   'Two composition rules cover most code: sequential phases add and the largest wins, nested work multiplies. Reading a function\u2019s complexity is applying those two rules to its loop structure.'],
 d:[['Dominant exponent of 5n\u00b2 + n','2'],['log\u2082(64)','6'],['n log\u2082 n at n = 4','8'],['n\u00b2 at n = 12','144'],['O(7) simplifies to O(?)','1']]},

loops: { an:'Counting loop iterations is counting cells in a shape: a single loop is a line, a nested loop is a square, a triangular loop is half a square. The area of the shape is the running time.',
 ex:['A loop\u2019s cost is how many times its body runs. A single pass over n items is n. Two independent nested loops give n \u00d7 n, because every outer iteration pays for a full inner pass.',
   'The triangular loop \u2014 for i in 1..n, for j in 1..i \u2014 runs 1 + 2 + \u22ef + n = n(n+1)/2 times. That is the arithmetic series from the precalculus track, and it is the exact count behind every all-pairs comparison.',
   'A loop that halves its variable runs log\u2082 n times, because that is how many halvings reduce n to 1. Any loop that multiplies or divides its counter is logarithmic, not linear \u2014 the single most useful pattern to recognise on sight.',
   'Combinations follow the two rules: sequential loops add (largest wins), nested loops multiply. A linear loop containing a halving loop is n log n, which is why heap sort and merge sort share that bound.'],
 d:[['Nested n\u00d7n iterations at n = 5','25'],['\u03a3 i for i = 1..10','55'],['Halvings from 32 down to 1','5'],['n(n+1)/2 at n = 8','36'],['Iterations of a single loop, n = 9','9']]},

recurrences: { an:'A divide-and-conquer recurrence is an org chart: each manager splits the work between two reports. The height of the chart is the number of halvings, and the total cost is work per level times the number of levels.',
 ex:['A recurrence expresses an algorithm\u2019s cost in terms of the cost of its recursive calls. T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n reads: solve two half-size problems, then spend n combining them \u2014 which is merge sort exactly.',
   'The reliable solving method is the recursion tree. Halving gives log\u2082 n levels. At each level of merge sort, the merges total n work, so the whole tree costs n \u00d7 log n.',
   'Binary search\u2019s recurrence T(n) = T(n/2) + 1 has one branch, not two: the same log n levels but constant work per level, giving O(log n) total. The number of branches matters as much as the split.',
   'Correctness of the recursion itself is the strong induction from the discrete track: assume the recursive calls are right for all smaller sizes, show the combine step preserves it, and check the base case stops it.'],
 d:[['Levels for n = 64, halving','6'],['n log\u2082 n at n = 8','24'],['Binary search comparisons, n = 256','8'],['log\u2082(128)','7'],['Levels for n = 1024, halving','10']]},

develop: { an:'Designing an algorithm is navigating without GPS: first state exactly where you are and where you must get, walk the dumb route once so you know one exists, then look for the shortcut the terrain offers.',
 ex:['Step one is restating the problem precisely: what are the inputs, what exactly must come out, and what are the sizes? Most wrong algorithms solve a slightly different problem than the one asked.',
   'Step two is a brute force, written or at least sized. Enumerating the whole space gives you a correctness oracle and a cost estimate. If the space is 10\u2076 you may already be done; if it is 2\u2075\u2070 you know a structural idea is mandatory.',
   'Step three is the mathematical one: find the property the brute force ignores. Sorted input admits binary search. Overlapping subproblems admit dynamic programming. A safe local choice admits a greedy algorithm. The property comes first; the paradigm is just its name.',
   'Step five closes the loop: argue correctness with an invariant or induction, state the Big-O, and only then optimise constants. Optimising before proving is how fast wrong answers get shipped.'],
 d:[['Pairs among 10 items','45'],['Subsets of 20 items: 2 to what power','20'],['Sorted input suggests which search','binary'],['C(6,2)','15'],['2\u00b9\u2070','1024']]},

amortized: { an:'Amortised analysis is a coffee card: nine cheap stamps prepay the free tenth cup. The barista never loses money on the promotion because the accounting guarantees every expensive cup was already paid for by the cheap ones before it.',
 ex:['Some data structures are cheap almost always and expensive occasionally. Quoting the worst single operation (O(n) for a resizing append) is misleading; quoting the average over any sequence \u2014 the amortised cost \u2014 is both honest and provable.',
   'The doubling array is the canonical case: resizes copy 1 + 2 + 4 + \u22ef + n elements, a geometric series summing below 2n. Spread over n appends, that is under 2 copies each \u2014 amortised O(1), courtesy of the series from the precalculus track.',
   'The accounting method makes it rigorous: charge every cheap operation a constant \u201ctax\u201d banked as credit, and pay for each expensive operation entirely from the bank. If the balance never goes negative, the amortised bound holds for every possible sequence.',
   'Amortised is not average-case: it is a worst-case guarantee on totals, with no probability involved. A hash table\u2019s O(1) average can be defeated by adversarial keys; a doubling array\u2019s amortised O(1) cannot be defeated by any input.'],
 d:[['Copies growing 1\u21922\u2192\u22ef\u219264','63'],['Copies growing 1\u21922\u2192\u22ef\u219216','15'],['Amortised appends for n=1000: O(?)','1'],['Is amortised probabilistic? 1 yes 0 no','0'],['1+2+4+8+16','31']]},

pnp: { an:'P versus NP is the gap between grading and writing. Checking a finished proof, a filled Sudoku, a seating plan takes minutes; producing one can take a lifetime. The open question is whether that gap is real or an illusion of our ignorance.',
 ex:['P is the class of problems solvable in polynomial time \u2014 sorting, shortest paths, matching. NP is the class whose solutions can be VERIFIED in polynomial time, given the answer. Every P problem is trivially in NP; whether the containment is strict is the P vs NP question, open since 1971.',
   'NP-complete problems \u2014 SAT, TSP, knapsack, graph colouring, generalised Sudoku \u2014 are the hardest in NP: a fast algorithm for any one gives a fast algorithm for all, via reductions that translate one problem into another.',
   'Proving your problem NP-hard is actionable intelligence: stop hunting for a fast exact algorithm. Reach for approximation (provably near-optimal), heuristics (fast in practice), restricted inputs, or hand the instance to an industrial SAT solver \u2014 which routinely cracks million-variable instances despite the worst case.',
   'Modern cryptography lives in this gap deliberately: RSA is easy to verify and believed hard to invert. If P = NP, one-way functions collapse and most deployed cryptography goes with them \u2014 one more reason the $1M Millennium Prize remains unclaimed.'],
 d:[['Is sorting in P? 1 yes 0 no','1'],['Fast to check, hard to solve: which class','NP'],['SAT solvable fast \u21d2 all NP fast? 1 yes 0 no','1'],['Is P inside NP? 1 yes 0 no','1'],['Year the question was posed','1971']]},

'binary-search': { an:'Binary search is guessing a number between 1 and 100 where each guess is answered "higher" or "lower". Guessing 50 first is not luck \u2014 it is the choice that discards half the possibilities no matter which answer comes back.',
 ex:['Binary search maintains a range [lo, hi] that must contain the target if it is present at all. That sentence is the loop invariant, and every step preserves it: compare against the midpoint, and discard the half that cannot contain the answer.',
   'Sortedness is what licenses the discard. If the midpoint is smaller than the target, every element to its left is also smaller, so the whole left half goes. Without order, one comparison tells you about exactly one element.',
   'The midpoint is written lo + (hi \u2212 lo)/2 rather than (lo + hi)/2 because the latter can overflow when both indices are large. This bug sat in the Java standard library for nine years before anyone noticed.',
   'The general lesson is bigger than arrays. Any monotonic predicate can be binary searched: the smallest buffer size that fits, the lowest rate limit that holds, the earliest commit that broke the build. Git bisect is binary search over history.'],
 d:[['Comparisons for n = 64','6'],['Comparisons for n = 1,000,000','20'],['Comparisons for n = 128','7'],['Range size after 3 halvings of 80','10'],['Comparisons for n = 16','4']]},

sorting: { an:'Merge sort is merging two already-sorted piles of paper by repeatedly taking the smaller top sheet. Quicksort is picking one sheet and throwing everything smaller to the left, everything larger to the right, then repeating on each side.',
 ex:['Merge sort splits the array in half, sorts each half recursively, and merges the two sorted halves in linear time. Its recurrence T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n gives log n levels of n work, so O(n log n) in every case \u2014 no bad inputs exist.',
   'Quicksort partitions around a pivot so that everything smaller sits left and everything larger sits right, then recurses on both sides with no merge step. Balanced pivots give the same O(n log n); a pathological pivot gives O(n\u00b2), which random pivot choice makes vanishingly unlikely.',
   'In practice quicksort usually wins because it sorts in place with sequential memory access, while merge sort allocates a buffer and copies. Merge sort keeps its edge where stability or guaranteed worst case matters, and it is the natural choice for data too large for memory.',
   'No comparison sort can beat \u03a9(n log n), because n! possible orderings need at least log\u2082(n!) \u2248 n log n yes/no answers to distinguish. Counting sort and radix sort go faster only by not comparing at all.'],
 d:[['Levels in merge sort, n = 256','8'],['n log\u2082 n at n = 16','64'],['Merge sort worst case exponent of n\u00b2? enter 0 if none','0'],['Quicksort worst case at n = 100 as n\u00b2','10000'],['Levels in merge sort, n = 64','6']]},

hashing: { an:'A hash table is a coat check. Instead of searching every hook for your coat, the ticket number sends you straight to one hook. Collisions are two coats assigned the same hook \u2014 rare if the numbering is good, catastrophic if everyone gets hook 7.',
 ex:['A hash function turns a key of any shape into a number, and that number modulo the table size gives a bucket index. Because array indexing is constant time, lookup skips searching entirely \u2014 this is the whole trick.',
   'Collisions are inevitable: infinitely many keys map into finitely many buckets. Chaining stores a small list per bucket; open addressing probes for the next free slot. Both are fine while the load factor \u03b1 = n/m stays low, which is why tables resize at around 0.75.',
   'The birthday paradox from the probability track explains why collisions arrive sooner than intuition suggests: with m buckets you expect a collision after roughly \u221am insertions, not m. A 64-bit hash starts colliding around 2\u00b3\u00b2 items.',
   'Worst-case lookup is O(n) when every key lands in one bucket. That is not merely theoretical \u2014 hash-flooding attacks deliberately construct such keys, which is why production hash functions are seeded randomly at process start.'],
 d:[['Load factor: 24 entries, 32 buckets','0.75'],['Bucket for hash 47 in 16 buckets','15'],['Average lookup cost as O(?)','1'],['Bucket for hash 100 in 8 buckets','4'],['Entries at \u03b1 = 0.5 with 64 buckets','32']]},

'graph-algos': { an:'BFS is a flood spreading outward one street at a time; the first time water reaches a house, it took the shortest route. Dijkstra is the same flood where some streets are slower, so you always extend from the cheapest frontier point.',
 ex:['BFS uses a queue, so vertices come out in order of edge-distance from the source. The first time it reaches a vertex, it has used the fewest possible edges \u2014 that is the shortest-path guarantee, and it holds only when all edges cost the same.',
   'DFS uses a stack and dives deep before backtracking. Its finishing order is what produces topological sorts, its back-edges reveal cycles, and its restart count gives the number of connected components. Same O(V + E), completely different uses.',
   'Dijkstra generalises BFS by replacing the queue with a priority queue keyed on distance so far. It always finalises the cheapest unfinished vertex, which is a greedy choice that is provably safe when no edge is negative.',
   'That proof breaks with negative edges: a vertex finalised early might be improvable later through a negative detour. Bellman-Ford relaxes every edge V\u22121 times instead, costing O(V\u00b7E) but tolerating negative weights and detecting negative cycles.'],
 d:[['BFS cost with V=50, E=200','250'],['Queue or stack for BFS','queue'],['Queue or stack for DFS','stack'],['Does Dijkstra allow negative edges? 1 yes 0 no','0'],['BFS cost with V=20, E=30','50']]},

dp: { fb:"Two scribes were asked how many paths cross a great lattice. The first walked every path, and his count outlived him. The second walked to each crossing once, wrote the number of ways to reach it on the stone, and computed each new stone from two stones already written. She finished by supper. The king asked her secret. The problem repeats itself inside itself, she said. My predecessor solved each repetition afresh. I solve it once and read the stone. Her method needed nothing but the humility to write things down.", an:'Dynamic programming is showing your working on scrap paper and then reusing it. Naive recursive Fibonacci recomputes fib(30) millions of times; writing each answer down once turns an exponential tree into a single pass.',
 ex:['DP applies when two conditions hold: optimal substructure, meaning an optimal solution is built from optimal solutions of subproblems, and overlapping subproblems, meaning the same subproblem recurs. Without overlap, plain divide and conquer is enough.',
   'Top-down memoisation keeps the natural recursion and adds a cache checked on entry. Bottom-up tabulation fills a table in dependency order, avoiding recursion overhead and stack depth. They compute the same values; the choice is ergonomic.',
   'Cost is states times work per state. Fibonacci has n states at constant work, giving O(n). Edit distance has n\u00b7m states at constant work, giving O(nm) \u2014 which is exactly what your diff tool runs when it compares two files.',
   'The hard part is never the code, it is identifying the state: the minimal information that distinguishes one subproblem from another. Once the state is right, the recurrence usually writes itself.'],
 d:[['Naive fib is O(2\u207f); memoised is O(n). States for n=40','40'],['Edit distance states, 10\u00d720 strings','200'],['DP cost: 50 states \u00d7 4 work','200'],['Does DP need overlapping subproblems? 1 yes 0 no','1'],['Fib(10)','55']]},

greedy: { an:'A greedy algorithm is packing a bag by always putting in the most valuable item that still fits. Sometimes that is optimal, sometimes it wastes the last bit of space \u2014 and the only way to know which is to prove it.',
 ex:['A greedy algorithm makes the locally best choice at each step and never reconsiders. It is the simplest and fastest strategy available, so it is worth trying first \u2014 but only worth shipping once you can prove the greedy choice is safe.',
   'The standard proof is an exchange argument: take any optimal solution, show you can swap its first choice for the greedy one without making it worse, and induct. If that swap is always possible, greedy is optimal.',
   'It provably works for interval scheduling by earliest finish time, Huffman coding, Kruskal\u2019s and Prim\u2019s MST, and Dijkstra. Each of those has an exchange argument behind it, not a hunch.',
   'It fails on 0/1 knapsack, on coin systems like {1, 3, 4}, and on longest path. The failures are quiet: the code runs and returns a plausible but suboptimal answer, which is why the proof is the deliverable, not the loop.'],
 d:[['Coins {1,5,10,25}: fewest for 40','3'],['Coins {1,3,4}: optimal for 6','2'],['Coins {1,5,10,25}: fewest for 99','9'],['Is greedy optimal for 0/1 knapsack? 1 yes 0 no','0'],['Coins {1,5,10,25}: fewest for 15','2']]},

factors: { an:'Prime factors are the ingredient list of a number. Two numbers share a common factor the way two recipes share an ingredient, and the greatest common factor is the largest batch you could make from what both have.',
 ex:['One number divides another when the division leaves remainder zero. That is the same test as a mod check returning 0, which is how you write it in code.',
   'The divisibility shortcuts come from base 10. A number is even if its last digit is, because 10 is even. It is divisible by 3 or 9 exactly when its digit sum is, because 10 leaves remainder 1 when divided by either.',
   'Every integer above 1 factors into primes in exactly one way. Writing 24 as 2\u00b3 \u00b7 3 and 36 as 2\u00b2 \u00b7 3\u00b2 makes the shared part visible: take the lower power of each shared prime and you have the GCF, 2\u00b2 \u00b7 3 = 12.',
   'The GCF reduces fractions and the LCM finds common denominators. Both come back in the number theory track, where Euclid\u2019s algorithm computes the GCF without factoring anything at all.'],
 d:[['GCF of 18 and 24','6'],['LCM of 4 and 10','20'],['Is 471 divisible by 3? 1 yes 0 no','1'],['GCF of 15 and 25','5'],['Prime factors of 20: how many distinct','2']]},

decimals: { an:'A percent is a fraction wearing a uniform: the denominator is always 100, so any two percentages can be compared at a glance. That standardisation is why every benchmark reports one.',
 ex:['Percent means per hundred, so 35% is 35/100 = 0.35. Converting to a decimal first turns every percent problem into ordinary multiplication.',
   'Percent of a number is a single multiplication: 15% of 240 is 0.15 \u00d7 240 = 36. Percent change is (new \u2212 old)/old \u00d7 100, and the denominator must be the original value \u2014 dividing by the new one is the single most common reporting error.',
   'Percentages do not compose by addition. A 50% drop followed by a 50% rise does not return you to the start; it leaves you at 75% of the original, because each percent applies to a different base.',
   'Decimals in binary floating point are not exact. One tenth has no finite binary representation, so 0.1 + 0.2 evaluates to 0.30000000000000004 in essentially every language. Money is stored in integer cents for exactly this reason.'],
 d:[['15% of 240','36'],['25% of 80','20'],['200 \u2192 150 is what % decrease','25'],['0.4 as a percent','40'],['10% of 350','35']]},

ratios: { an:'A rate is a ratio normalised to one unit, which is what makes two systems comparable. Requests per second, miles per gallon and cost per user are the same move: divide until the denominator is 1.',
 ex:['A ratio compares two quantities. It is not a count \u2014 a 2:3 ratio holds whether the actual numbers are 2 and 3 or 200 and 300, because scaling both parts preserves it.',
   'A proportion states that two ratios are equal, and cross-multiplication solves it: a/b = c/d becomes ad = bc, turning a fraction equation into a linear one.',
   'A unit rate divides until the denominator is 1. Three hundred requests in twelve seconds becomes 25 requests per second, which you can compare directly against any other server.',
   'Scaling a system is a proportion. If three servers handle 900 users, five handle 1500 at the same rate \u2014 assuming linear scaling, which real systems only approximate before contention sets in.'],
 d:[['300 in 12 seconds: rate per second','25'],['3 servers \u2192 900 users. 5 servers?','1500'],['Solve 2/5 = x/20','8'],['Simplify the ratio 12:18 (enter as a:b)','2:3'],['240 miles in 4 hours: mph','60']]},

expressions: { an:'An expression is a formula with holes in it, exactly like a function body before you pass arguments. Simplifying is constant folding; substituting is calling it.',
 ex:['A variable is a named placeholder. An expression combines variables, numbers and operations but contains no equals sign \u2014 it is a value waiting to be computed, not a claim.',
   'Like terms share the same variable at the same power, and only those can be combined. 4x + 2x is 6x, but 6x and \u22124 stay separate because they measure different things. This is the same reason you cannot add seconds to bytes.',
   'Distribution removes a bracket: a(b + c) = ab + ac. Every term inside gets multiplied, and forgetting the second one is the most frequent algebra slip there is.',
   'Substitution replaces the letter with a value and then evaluates using ordinary order of operations. Substituting first and simplifying after is the safe order; doing it the other way is where sign errors creep in.'],
 d:[['Simplify 4x + 2x','6x'],['Evaluate 3x + 2 at x = 5','17'],['Expand 2(x + 4): constant term','8'],['Evaluate x\u00b2 \u2212 x at x = 4','12'],['Simplify 7y \u2212 3y','4y']]},

inequalities: { an:'An inequality is a guard clause. "x must be at least 0 and less than the array length" is a compound inequality, and it is the exact condition a bounds check evaluates on every access.',
 ex:['An inequality describes a range rather than a point. Adding or subtracting the same value from both sides leaves the direction unchanged, exactly as with equations.',
   'Multiplying or dividing by a negative number flips the direction. \u22122x > 6 becomes x < \u22123, not x > \u22123. Miss the flip and your answer set is precisely the complement of the correct one \u2014 a bug that passes casual inspection.',
   'Compound inequalities like 2 \u2264 x < 9 impose two constraints at once. That half-open shape, inclusive at the bottom and exclusive at the top, is the same convention as range(2, 9) and array indexing, and it is deliberate: the length of the range is simply the difference.',
   'Interval notation writes [a, b] for inclusive endpoints and (a, b) for exclusive. Mixing them, as in [0, n), is how the standard array bounds condition is written in mathematics.'],
 d:[['Solve \u22123x > 9 (enter the boundary)','-3',['\u22123']],['Integers with 2 \u2264 x < 9','7'],['Integers with 0 \u2264 x < 5','5'],['Solve x + 4 < 10 (boundary)','6'],['Solve 2x \u2265 14 (boundary)','7']]},

coordinates: { an:'The coordinate plane is a screen. A point is a pixel address, and the only difference is that screens flip the y-axis so it grows downward \u2014 which is why sprites appear upside down when you forget.',
 ex:['An ordered pair (x, y) locates a point by horizontal distance first, then vertical. Order matters: (3, 5) and (5, 3) are different points, and the convention is fixed.',
   'The axes cut the plane into four quadrants, numbered counterclockwise from the top right. Reading the signs of a point tells you its quadrant without plotting anything.',
   'Distance between two points is Pythagoras applied to the differences: \u221a((x\u2082\u2212x\u2081)\u00b2 + (y\u2082\u2212y\u2081)\u00b2). This is the Euclidean distance used in clustering, collision detection and nearest-neighbour search.',
   'The midpoint averages both coordinates. Averaging is also how you find a centroid, which generalises directly to the k-means algorithm in machine learning.'],
 d:[['Distance from (0,0) to (3,4)','5'],['Distance from (0,0) to (6,8)','10'],['Midpoint x of (2,0) and (8,0)','5'],['Quadrant of (\u22122, \u22125)','3'],['Distance from (1,1) to (4,5)','5']]},

'word-problems': { an:'A word problem is a requirements document. "Twice as many entries as connections, plus a reserve of fifty" becomes 2c + 50, and getting that translation right is most of the work in both settings.',
 ex:['Start by naming the unknown explicitly. Writing "let u be the number of users" costs one line and prevents solving for the wrong quantity, which is the most common way these go wrong.',
   'Translate phrase by phrase. "Of" means multiply, "is" means equals, "per" signals a rate. The dangerous phrase is "less than", which reverses the written order: seven less than twice a number is 2x \u2212 7, not 7 \u2212 2x.',
   'Most cost problems have the shape fixed + rate \u00d7 quantity, which is y = mx + b with different nouns. Recognising that shape means you already know how to solve it.',
   'Check by substituting back into the original sentence, not into your own equation. If your translation was wrong, checking against your equation will confirm the error rather than catch it.'],
 d:[['$40 + $3 per user = $130. Users?','30'],['Twice a number less 7 is 15. The number','11'],['3 times a number is 51. The number','17'],['$20 + $5n = $60. Find n','8'],['A number plus 12 equals 30','18']]},

quantifiers: { an:'A for-all is a unit test that must pass on every input; a there-exists is a search that only needs one hit. Negating a for-all does not give a for-all \u2014 it gives a single counterexample, which is exactly what a failing test reports.',
 ex:['A predicate is a statement with a variable in it, such as P(x) = "x is positive". It has no truth value until you either supply an x or quantify over a domain.',
   '\u2200x P(x) claims the predicate holds for every element of the domain; \u2203x P(x) claims it holds for at least one. Over a small domain you can settle either by checking each element.',
   'Negation swaps the quantifier and negates the predicate: \u00ac\u2200x P(x) \u2261 \u2203x \u00acP(x), and \u00ac\u2203x P(x) \u2261 \u2200x \u00acP(x). These are De Morgan\u2019s laws extended to infinite conjunctions and disjunctions, and they are why one counterexample refutes a universal claim.',
   'Order of nested quantifiers changes meaning. "Every user has a password" (\u2200u\u2203p) is ordinary; "there is one password all users share" (\u2203p\u2200u) is a security incident. Same symbols, opposite claims.'],
 d:[['Domain {1,2,3}: values where x > 1','2'],['Counterexamples to refute \u2200x P(x)','1'],['Negation of \u2200 is which symbol: enter exists or forall','exists'],['Domain {1,2,3,4}: values where x is even','2'],['Is \u2200x\u2203y the same as \u2203y\u2200x? 1 yes 0 no','0']]},

boolean: { an:'Boolean algebra is arithmetic where the only numbers are 0 and 1, and every simplification you find deletes a physical gate from a chip. Absorption is the rule that pays rent.',
 ex:['Boolean algebra operates on two values with AND written as multiplication, OR as addition, and NOT as an overbar. Most familiar arithmetic laws carry over, which is why the notation was chosen.',
   'Two rules have no arithmetic analogue and do most of the work. Domination says x + 1 = 1: an OR with a true term is true regardless. Absorption says x + xy = x, because factoring gives x(1 + y) = x \u00b7 1 = x, letting you delete an entire AND gate.',
   'De Morgan\u2019s laws move a negation across an operator, flipping it: (xy)\u0304 = x\u0304 + \u0233. In hardware this converts between NAND and NOR forms, which matters because NAND gates are cheaper to fabricate.',
   'Verification by truth table costs 2\u207f rows, so a 4-input function needs 16 and a 30-input function needs a billion. That exponential wall is why circuit verification uses algebraic methods and SAT solvers rather than enumeration.'],
 d:[['Simplify x + xy','x'],['x + 1 equals','1'],['x \u00b7 0 equals','0'],['Truth table rows for 4 inputs','16'],['x + x\u0304 equals','1']]},

relations: { an:'A relation is a table of pairs, like a foreign-key column. Transitivity is whether "depends on" chains \u2014 if it does, you can precompute the full closure once instead of walking the graph on every query.',
 ex:['A relation on a set is just a subset of its ordered pairs. With 3 elements there are 9 possible pairs and therefore 2\u2079 = 512 possible relations, most of which are structureless. The named properties pick out the useful ones.',
   'Reflexive means every element relates to itself. Symmetric means direction does not matter. Antisymmetric means the only two-way pairs are self-pairs, which is what orderings need. Transitive means chains collapse: a to b and b to c implies a to c.',
   'Reflexive, symmetric and transitive together make an equivalence relation, which partitions the set into disjoint classes. Equality, congruence mod n, and "same connected component" are all equivalences, and each one carves the set into buckets.',
   'Reflexive, antisymmetric and transitive make a partial order. Build dependencies form one, which is exactly why a topological sort exists \u2014 and why a cycle in the dependency graph breaks antisymmetry and makes the build unorderable.'],
 d:[['Ordered pairs in A\u00d7A for |A| = 3','9'],['Ordered pairs in A\u00d7A for |A| = 5','25'],['Is < transitive? 1 yes 0 no','1'],['Is < reflexive? 1 yes 0 no','0'],['Properties needed for an equivalence relation','3']]},

recurrence: { fb:"A monk asked how long the sixty-four golden discs would take, moving one at a time. The abbot said only: to move a tower, first move the tower above its base, then the base, then the tower again. The monk wrote the rule as a number: each tower costs twice the smaller tower and one more. He unrolled it — 1, 3, 7, 15 — and saw the pattern two-to-the-n minus one. At sixty-four discs he stopped counting in moves and started counting in ages of the universe. Define the step, find the closed form, and you may learn the answer is: never.", an:'A recurrence is a rule for the next step written in terms of the last, like a loop body that reads the previous iteration\u2019s value. Solving it means finding a formula that skips straight to step n.',
 ex:['A recurrence defines a\u2099 in terms of earlier terms plus one or more base cases. Both parts are required: without the base case the sequence is undetermined, and without the rule it never extends.',
   'Unrolling is the first tool. Tower of Hanoi gives H\u2099 = 2H\u2099\u208b\u2081 + 1, and writing out 1, 3, 7, 15, 31 makes the pattern 2\u207f \u2212 1 obvious. Guessing a closed form then proving it by induction is a completely legitimate method.',
   'Linear homogeneous recurrences like a\u2099 = c\u2081a\u2099\u208b\u2081 + c\u2082a\u2099\u208b\u2082 are solved by the characteristic equation r\u00b2 = c\u2081r + c\u2082. Its roots give the exponential building blocks of the closed form, which is how Fibonacci acquires its golden-ratio formula.',
   'Divide-and-conquer recurrences T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n) are priced by the recursion tree or the master theorem. Merge sort with a = 2, b = 2, f(n) = n gives n log n, the single most-quoted result in the subject.'],
 d:[['Hanoi H\u2099 = 2\u207f \u2212 1 at n = 4','15'],['Hanoi at n = 6','63'],['Fibonacci F\u2087 with F\u2081=F\u2082=1','13'],['Fibonacci F\u2081\u2080','55'],['a\u2099 = 2a\u2099\u208b\u2081, a\u2081 = 3. Find a\u2084','24']]},

'advanced-counting': { an:'Pigeonhole is the coat-check argument: eleven coats and ten hooks means some hook holds two, no matter how carefully you assign them. It is the proof that hash collisions are not a design flaw but a mathematical certainty.',
 ex:['The pigeonhole principle says that placing n items into k boxes with n > k forces some box to hold at least two. It is obvious and enormously useful, because it converts a counting fact into a guarantee.',
   'The generalised form is sharper: some box holds at least \u2308n/k\u2309 items. Mapping 1000 keys into 100 buckets guarantees a bucket with 10 or more, regardless of how good the hash function is.',
   'Inclusion\u2013exclusion corrects double-counting. For two sets |A\u222aB| = |A| + |B| \u2212 |A\u2229B|; for three you add the singles, subtract the three pairwise intersections, then add the triple back. The alternating pattern continues for any number of sets.',
   'Stars and bars counts ways to distribute n identical items into k distinct bins as C(n+k\u22121, k\u22121). It answers "how many ways to split 10 requests across 3 servers" and similar allocation questions that permutations cannot.'],
 d:[['1000 keys, 100 buckets: guaranteed max in one','10'],['13 people, 12 months: min sharing a month','2'],['|A|=20, |B|=15, |A\u2229B|=5. |A\u222aB|','30'],['25 items, 6 boxes: some box has at least','5'],['|A|=8, |B|=8, disjoint. |A\u222aB|','16']]},

automata: { an:'A finite state machine is a board game with a token: you are always on exactly one square, and each input tells you which square to move to. With no dice and no notepad, it can never count higher than it has squares.',
 ex:['An FSM has a finite set of states, an alphabet of input symbols, a transition function, a start state and a set of accepting states. Its entire memory is which state it currently occupies \u2014 there is nothing else.',
   'Languages an FSM can recognise are exactly the regular languages, which are exactly what regular expressions describe. Every regex you write compiles to a state machine, which is why matching runs in linear time on well-behaved engines.',
   'That finiteness is a hard ceiling. Matching balanced parentheses to arbitrary depth requires counting how many are open, which needs unbounded memory. No FSM and no true regex can do it, which is why HTML and JSON need real parsers.',
   'The pumping lemma formalises the limit: any sufficiently long accepted string must contain a loop that can be repeated, and finding a string where repetition breaks membership proves the language is not regular.'],
 d:[['4 states, 2 symbols: transitions needed','8'],['3 states, 2 symbols: transitions needed','6'],['Can an FSM match a\u207fb\u207f? 1 yes 0 no','0'],['5 states, 3 symbols: transitions','15'],['Memory an FSM has beyond its state','0']]},

vectors: { an:'A vector is a set of dials. A song might be described by six dials \u2014 tempo, loudness, energy and so on \u2014 and two songs are similar when their dials sit in nearly the same positions. Embeddings do this with 768 dials instead of six.',
 ex:['A vector is an ordered list of numbers, and its length as a list is its dimensionality. Two dimensions can be drawn on paper, three can be imagined, and 768 cannot \u2014 but every formula keeps working regardless, which is the point.',
   'Addition and scalar multiplication work component by component. Adding vectors combines directions; multiplying by a scalar stretches without rotating. These two operations are what makes something a vector space.',
   'Magnitude generalises Pythagoras: \u2016v\u2016 = \u221a(\u03a3v\u1d62\u00b2), summing over however many components exist. Dividing a vector by its own magnitude gives a unit vector that keeps direction and discards length, which is what you want when only meaning matters and not intensity.',
   'In machine learning every input becomes a vector before anything else happens. An image is its pixel values, a word is a learned embedding, a user is their behaviour counts. Once everything is a vector, one set of mathematics handles all of it.'],
 d:[['Magnitude of [3, 4]','5'],['Magnitude of [6, 8]','10'],['[1,2] + [3,4]: first component','4'],['3 \u00d7 [2, 5]: second component','15'],['Dimensions of [1,2,3,4]','4']]},

dotproduct: { an:'The dot product is an agreement score. Two people rating the same ten films agree strongly when their high scores line up; the dot product adds up how much their opinions point the same way.',
 ex:['The dot product multiplies matching components and sums the results, collapsing two vectors into a single number. That number is large and positive when the vectors point the same way, near zero when they are perpendicular, and negative when they oppose.',
   'The geometric form u \u00b7 v = \u2016u\u2016\u2016v\u2016cos \u03b8 connects it to the trigonometry track. Rearranging gives cosine similarity: divide the dot product by both magnitudes and you get cos \u03b8 directly, a number between \u22121 and 1 that ignores length entirely.',
   'Ignoring length is exactly what you want for text. A long document and a short one about the same topic point in the same direction but have very different magnitudes, so cosine similarity finds them related while raw dot product would not.',
   'A neural network layer is thousands of dot products computed at once: each output neuron takes the dot product of the input with its own weight row. That is why GPUs, which multiply and add in bulk, transformed the field.'],
 d:[['[2,3] \u00b7 [4,1]','11'],['[1,0] \u00b7 [0,1]','0'],['[2,2] \u00b7 [3,3]','12'],['Cosine similarity of identical directions','1'],['[5,0] \u00b7 [2,7]','10']]},

matrices: { an:'A matrix is a machine with input and output ports. A 4\u00d73 matrix accepts a 3-wire plug and emits a 4-wire one. Chaining machines only works when the plug fits the socket, which is exactly the shape rule.',
 ex:['A matrix is a rectangular grid of numbers that acts as a function on vectors. Multiplying an m\u00d7n matrix by an n-vector produces an m-vector, so the matrix maps one space into another of possibly different size.',
   'The shape rule is absolute: (m\u00d7n)(n\u00d7p) is legal only when the inner dimensions match, and the result is m\u00d7p. Almost every shape error in model code is a violation of this one line.',
   'Each entry of the product is a dot product of a row with a column, so matrix multiplication is just many dot products arranged in a grid. It is not commutative \u2014 AB and BA generally differ and may not even both be defined \u2014 because applying transformations in a different order gives a different result.',
   'A neural network layer is y = Wx + b: one matrix multiply and one addition. A model with billions of parameters is billions of numbers inside these W matrices, which is why parameter count and matrix dimensions are the same conversation.'],
 d:[['(4\u00d73)(3\u00d75): result rows','4'],['(4\u00d73)(3\u00d75): result columns','5'],['Weights mapping 512 \u2192 256','131072'],['Weights mapping 100 \u2192 10','1000'],['Is AB = BA generally? 1 yes 0 no','0']]},

'gradient-descent': { fb:"A shepherd was lost on a mountain in fog so thick she could not see her feet's next step, only feel the tilt of the ground. She made a rule: feel the slope, step downhill, repeat. With small steps she reached the valley by morning. Her brother, impatient, took giant strides — each one crossed the valley floor and landed him up the far slope, back and forth until exhaustion. Their mother, who trained models in the village, embroidered the lesson: the direction is given to you free of charge; the whole art is the size of the step.", an:'Gradient descent is walking downhill in thick fog. You cannot see the valley, but you can feel which way the ground slopes under your feet, so you take a step that way and repeat. Step too far and you cross the valley and climb the far side.',
 ex:['The gradient \u2207L is the vector of partial derivatives of the loss with respect to every parameter. It points in the direction of steepest increase, so the update subtracts it: \u03b8 \u2190 \u03b8 \u2212 \u03b7\u2207L. That minus sign is the whole difference between learning and diverging.',
   'The learning rate \u03b7 scales the step. Too large and the step overshoots the minimum and lands higher on the opposite slope, producing a loss curve that oscillates or explodes. Too small and training converges correctly but takes far longer than the budget allows.',
   'Computing \u2207L for a deep network is the chain rule applied backwards through the layers, which is backpropagation. Every gradient is a long product of local derivatives, and when that product shrinks or grows uncontrollably you get the vanishing and exploding gradient problems.',
   'Convergence means the gradient has flattened to near zero, which is the critical-point condition from the calculus track. In practice the surface is not convex, so you settle in a good local minimum rather than the global one \u2014 and that turns out to be fine.'],
 d:[['\u03b8=10, grad=4, \u03b7=0.5. New \u03b8','8'],['\u03b8=5, grad=2, \u03b7=1. New \u03b8','3'],['\u03b8=20, grad=10, \u03b7=0.1. New \u03b8','19'],['Gradient at convergence','0'],['\u03b8=0, grad=\u22124, \u03b7=0.5. New \u03b8','2']]},

bayes: { fb:"A watchman's dog barked at thieves with wonderful accuracy — ninety-nine barks in a hundred were right about a thief, when a thief was there. One night it barked, and the watchman drew his sword. An old guard stopped him: in thirty years, how many nights had thieves? One in a thousand. So among a thousand barks, one is a thief and ten are foxes. The dog's skill was real; the town's thieves were rare; the bark alone was mostly fox. Weigh the evidence, said the guard, but weigh what was likely before the evidence, too.", an:'Bayes is how a doctor reads a test result. The result matters, but so does how common the disease is. A positive result for something vanishingly rare is usually a false alarm, and no amount of test accuracy changes that arithmetic.',
 ex:['Bayes\u2019 theorem reverses a conditional probability: P(A|B) = P(B|A)P(A)/P(B). It converts "how likely is this evidence if the hypothesis is true" into "how likely is the hypothesis given the evidence", which is the direction you actually need.',
   'The prior P(A) is your belief before the evidence and the posterior P(A|B) is your belief after. Neglecting the prior \u2014 the base rate fallacy \u2014 is the most consequential probability error in practice, and it is what makes a 99% accurate test for a 1-in-1000 disease produce mostly false positives.',
   'Work it with counts rather than percentages and it becomes obvious. Among 1000 people, 1 is sick and tests positive; about 10 healthy people also test positive. Eleven positives, one real. Around 9%.',
   'Naive Bayes classifiers apply this while assuming features are independent, which is almost never true. The assumption is wrong and the classifier works well anyway, which is a useful lesson about models in general.'],
 d:[['P(B|A)=0.8, P(A)=0.5, P(B)=0.4. P(A|B)','1'],['P(A) = 0.25. P(not A)','0.75'],['1 in 1000 sick, 99% test: rough % of positives that are real','9'],['P(B|A)=0.6, P(A)=0.5, P(B)=0.6. P(A|B)','0.5'],['Two independent events p=0.5 each: P(both)','0.25']]},

entropy: { fb:"A telegraph clerk charged by the symbol, and a miser hired him to report the sunrise daily. The clerk charged nothing: a message that is always the same carries no news, needs no wire. For a fair coin he charged one symbol flat. For the weather — mostly dry, rarely storms — he invented short codes for the common days and long ones for the rare, and beat every rival's prices. His secret ledger held one line: the price of a message is the surprise in it, and no code, however clever, sells surprise below cost.", an:'Entropy is the length of the message you would have to send. Announcing the outcome of a fair coin takes one bit; announcing that the sun rose takes none, because nobody was in doubt. Surprise is the thing being measured.',
 ex:['Entropy H = \u2212\u03a3 p\u1d62 log\u2082 p\u1d62 is the average number of bits needed to record which outcome occurred. A certain outcome carries zero entropy, and a uniform distribution over n outcomes carries the maximum, log\u2082 n.',
   'This is also the theoretical floor on compression. No lossless compressor can average fewer bits per symbol than the source entropy, which is why already-compressed files barely shrink when zipped again \u2014 the redundancy is already gone.',
   'Cross-entropy \u2212\u03a3 y\u1d62 log(\u0177\u1d62) measures how far a predicted distribution sits from the true one, and it is the loss function behind essentially every classifier. For a single correct class it reduces to \u2212log of the probability the model gave the right answer.',
   'That logarithm is why confident errors are punished so hard. Assigning 0.001 to the true class costs about 6.9, while an uncertain 0.5 costs 0.69 \u2014 ten times less. The loss function is explicitly teaching the model to be calibrated, not merely correct.'],
 d:[['Entropy of a fair coin in bits','1'],['Entropy of a certain outcome','0'],['Max entropy over 8 equally likely outcomes','3'],['Max entropy over 4 outcomes','2'],['Entropy of a fair 16-sided die','4']]},

};
